Civil rights groups issue travel warning for Missouri
As a result of discriminatory policies and racist attacks in Missouri, the NAACP issued a travel warning to people of color who were preparing to travel to Missouri.
“The NAACP Missouri travel advisory, effective August 28, 2017, calls for all African-American travelers, tourists, and Missourians to pay particular attention and take an extremely cautious approach when traveling in Missouri because of a recent series of suspicious racial events in Missouri,” the group’s statement said in a statement.
NAACP pointed out that a recently passed Missouri law makes it more difficult for people to win discrimination lawsuits, and that the state’s law enforcement also targets minority groups in some way, which has prompted the organization to issue travel warnings.
Violation of civil rights is taking place.
“They were interrogated, beaten, or killed because of their skin color,” Rod Chappell, president of NAACP in Missouri, told The Kansas City Star.
“We’ve received an unprecedented number of complaints.”
This is the organization's first such warning against a state in the United States.
The group cites racial slurs from the University of Missouri on black students and the death of 28-year-old Tennessee black male Tory Sanders.
Earlier this year, Sanders died under suspicious circumstances. He had run out of fuel while traveling in Missouri and was detained by Missouri police on a crime without charge.
A recent report from the Missouri Attorney General’s Office shows that “black drivers in the state are 75 percent more likely to be investigated for parking compared to whites.”
“This consultation is intended to raise people’s awareness of what could happen to their families, friends, and colleagues in Missouri.”
“People need to be prepared, whether it’s taking bail to Missouri or letting relatives know they’re traveling within the state.”
According to the FBI’s hate crime reporting program, Missouri recorded 100 hate crimes in 2015; the state ranks 16th in the nation, based on the number of crimes.
The travel warning is also a response to Missouri’s new law, which would make it more difficult to sue businesses for housing or employment discrimination.
U.S. states have passed immigration enforcement laws that require local law enforcement to detain immigration violators, and the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) said the move will increase the number of racial lawsuits and issue travel advisories for Texas and Arizona.
Travel advisories are usually issued by the U.S. government against other countries, but more recently, advisory groups have taken this approach in response to certain laws and trends within the United States.
