The BBC called Beijing the most exciting city: let foreigners feel "living in a new era"
Beijing may have a long history for three thousand years, but it is also rapidly transforming itself into today’s modern charismatic city.
With an amazingly fast Internet, with cutting-edge technologies such as face recognition software, a huge investment in artificial intelligence and an unrivalled international energy, it is one of the most exciting cities for exploratory foreigners.
According to the British Broadcasting Corporation website on January 16, the German Diaspora Clemens Sesch said: "If I can be successful there, I can succeed anywhere in the future." He quoted singer Frank Sinatra singing the lyrics of New York City.
SÉshi, a creative director at Traveler’s Archives, said: “If Sinatra lived to this day, he would most likely be singing about a city like Beijing,” he said, adding that living in the city will make you feel “living in a new era” and will always be able to keep up with the trends.
In an article published on 4Corners7Seas, a personal blog website based in English but recently living in Beijing, it's hard to say, "It's hard to tell, but it's hard to say what it's going to be. They want to see what it's going to look like, and they want to witness it for themselves."
The city’s high-quality, high-speed internet and a technology-friendly environment have made everyday life easier than ever.
“Beijing is rapidly transforming itself into a cashless society, and the Internet integrates the functionality of traditional wallets into smartphones, making it easier for everyone to make many transactions,” said Manila, who is currently the deputy editor-in-chief.
But smartphones are only the beginning of Beijing’s technological ambitions, facial recognition software has become so widespread that many mobile phone apps use the technology for bank transfers and payments, give people access to apartments and offices, and even use it to verify the identities of ride-hailing drivers.
Beijing also recently announced plans to spend 13.8 billion yuan to build an artificial intelligence science and technology park, where more than 400 companies will focus on the development of various artificial intelligence technologies ranging from biometrics to self-driving cars.
Food is also at the core of many major festivals in China over the years, participating in these festivities is respected by foreigners, the Spring Festival is the most important social and economic festival, and it is also one of the easiest festivals for foreigners to participate in.
“It’s just as important to help my Chinese friends prepare for the New Year, to clean the apartment and to paste the New Year’s greetings at the door of the Red Spring Federation. On New Year’s Eve, all the family members gather for a big, heart-to-date meal until midnight to greet the New Year with gorgeous fireworks. For foreigners, spending the night with friends and family is indeed an opportunity to integrate.”
There is a large and growing number of foreigners in Beijing, but China’s language barriers, as well as the ease of communication among expatriates, can easily become an excuse that they are not fully integrated into the local culture.
“Some foreigners have been living in China for years, but they have little experience with their host country. If your initial attempts to reach out to the Chinese are not successful, then it’s easy for you to get along easily with other foreigners.”
In these so-called "foreign bubbles" ("foreign bubbles" means that foreigners only mix between foreigners and do not come into contact with locals and local cultures), foreigners gather in their own communities, eat in Western restaurants, and have only foreigners among their friends.
The easiest way to overcome this is to learn a bit of Mandarin—it’s easier than one might think.
“One or two hours a week, or an entry-level radio drama, should be on everyone’s busy schedule.”
Even learning a few classic phrases can be helpful for shopping, ordering meals in restaurants, or gossiping in a taxi.
Recalcitrant air pollution is also a fact in daily life, but in recent years the government has tried to curb some of the problems.
Residents stressed the importance of going outdoors in warmer months, which tends to be less polluting, and to use those blue sky days as an opportunity to linger in historic alleys or to explore the popular kamikaze parks.
Norton said: "Beijing is the Chinese city where you can find the most things below the surface, and you can find very good art and music activities, indulgent nightlife, historical wonders, ultra-modern business areas and ancient alley areas."
