According to the European Commission, the move is detrimental to the interests of consumers and hinders competition.
EU regulators initially asked Google to respond by July 27.
If the anti-monopoly is convicted, Google faces a fine of up to 10% of its global revenue, about $7.4 billion.
Google didn’t immediately comment.
Google is also facing an investigation in the European Union for favoring its own shopping services in search results.
