I can understand the legal status of Bitcoin all over the world.
In addition to the price correction naturally triggered by speculative attributes, the current round of digital currency falls is also associated with the “enemy” – regulatory risks.
Judging from the visual map, the attitude of Eastern countries towards Bitcoin is more conservative than in the West, and the countries accounting for the main body are still watching the legitimacy of the digital currency, representing the "black swan risk" is still very large.
*This article is from Wall Street (WeChat ID: wallstreetcn), editor Wang Mu. For more exciting information, please go to wallstreet.com, or download the Wall Street Ensemble APP.
With the regulatory turmoil in major bitcoin markets such as China, South Korea and the European Union, the global digital currency led by bitcoin has been bloody this week, and the price of bitcoin fell below the psychological integer level of 10,000 US dollars on Tuesday and the largest one-day decline in three years.
In addition to the price pullback naturally triggered by speculative attributes, the collapse of the current round of digital currency is also related to the "enemy" - regulatory risks.
At this important juncture, howMuch.net, a visual big data website, has produced an atlas that shows Bitcoin’s legitimacy and degree of regulation in various countries around the world.
According to the following figure, green represents the legal market of bitcoin; orange represents the neutral market, although it has not yet publicly legalized the status of bitcoin, but there is no major restrictions on its use; light pink represents the restricted market, that is, the local government tries to use the digital currency through red line policy or regulation; dark pink represents a country that is defined as the illegality of Bitcoin; gray is a country that has not yet commented.
(Pictured above from HowMuch.Net visualization team on January 15)
The article pointed out that it can be seen from the distribution of green blocks in the figure that the attitude of Eastern countries to Bitcoin in the traditional sense is more conservative than in the West.
At present, Russia is the largest country that defines bitcoin as illegal, China is one of the largest restricted markets for Bitcoin, and India is one of the largest countries that has not yet issued relevant policies for Bitcoin.
North America and Western Europe are the regions with the highest levels of Bitcoin acceptance, where the distribution of green blocks is more concentrated.
The views of countries in the Middle East and Latin America on Bitcoin are clearly differentiated, and the distribution of color blocks in these regions is more mixed.
Among them, Iraq, Iran, Turkey and Brazil are the legal bitcoin markets, Afghanistan, Pakistan, Saudi Arabia, Egypt, Bolivia and other countries have a certain degree of restrictions on their transactions and use, and more Latin American countries have not yet voiced their views on Bitcoin.
A total of 99 of the world’s 246 (or 40 per cent) do not impose restrictions on the trading and use of bitcoin, i.e. in green and roosters.
There are 7 countries (or 3%) that are restricted markets (light pink), 10 countries (or 4%) define bitcoin as illegal (dark pink), and 130 countries (or 53%) don’t yet treat digital currencies any more (gray).
This shows that the countries that account for the majority of the world's entities (53%, more than half) are still waiting to see the legitimacy of the digital currency led by Bitcoin in China, and it also means that Bitcoin's "black swan risk" is still very large, because some of these countries may eventually make a legislative decision to restrict the digital currency.
Global regulators do not yet fully understand the emerging category of digital currency, which may be the main reason why most countries have not yet made a statement.
Although Bitcoin's popularity has increased exponentially since last year, it does not mean that all countries are interested, and some countries may be worried that the local fiat currency will therefore be threatened, or even more worried that the digital currency will be used by criminal organizations.
Wall Street News has written a detailed summary of the international regulatory news against bitcoin since last week, including China began to crack down on over-the-Counter exchanges of centralized service online platforms and mobile apps, South Korea is still considering the closure of digital currency exchanges, the European Union believes that bitcoin has not yet been widely accepted, and warned investors about the risk of “losing nothing to lose money”.
The editor-in-chief of Wall Street has mentioned that from any point of view of supervision, acceptance/criticism and adoption, 2018 will become the first year of digital currency for global central banks.
Major powers such as Russia have criticized bitcoin’s legal status, in fact, in order to give way to the official issuance of digital currency.
