Is there anything sacred?
Now Trump’s White House is targeting the Statue of Liberty
One of the surest signs of regime change were when they started attacking statues.
The United States, like everyone else, agreed. So Saddam Hussein in Baghdad’s Paradise Square in 2003 was orchestrated toppled.
As one of the Trump administration’s key thinkers, Stephen Miller, in a heated debate with Jim Acosta on the president’s proposal to severely restrict legal immigration, he’s certainly aware of what will happen to the Statue of Liberty cutting out a symbolic accumulator.
It’s fair to say that Miller didn’t attack the statue itself.
If he had done so, then a group of shipowners and libertarian followers were bound to devour him.
However, he did attack the significance of the statue, especially in 1903 when the famous poetry of Emma Lazarus was engraved on a pedestal (the monument was built 17 years later).
“I don’t want to talk about all the facts of history here, but the Statue of Liberty is a symbol of American freedom that illuminates the world.”
“The poem you’re referring to was added later, and it wasn’t part of the original Statue of Liberty.”
In fact, Miller was right, but as he himself put it, it wasn’t really about history.
This relates to the modern echo of the world-shattering words of Lazarus, the only country that seems to invite not only any old immigrants, but also the poorest of the poor: “your exhaustion, your poverty, or the desire of your crowded people to breathe for freedom,/Suffering people reject your crowded beaches.”
There’s no such thing as a “suffering person’s rejection” at a more dazzling moment on our screen.
Miller, who hasn’t worked in literary criticism, made it clear that these people wouldn’t be popular in Trump-era America.
This conflict over the meaning of the statue is part of a broader political and cultural war: it’s actually in conflict with the American ethos.
Lazarus, as well as her friends, who had engraved her verse on a pedestal after her death, were well aware that the behavior they were involved in was highly political.
The purpose of the sculpture is to express the connection between French and American republicanism through an ancient classical figuration that acts as the female incarnation of the Statue of Liberty.
Lazarus changed the meaning: in her poems, the female image is no longer abstract, she wants to speak.
She gave herself a very different name: the mother of exile.
The combination of the poem and the painting was a successful feminist coup d’état and a devastating blow to American nativism.
Just as Lazarus changed the meaning of the statue, the right-wingers always wanted to change it back to its original meaning.
Rush Limbaugh, a baptizer of Trump’s arrival, argued in 2010 that liberalism wasn’t an invitation to anyone, and he was now an early neoconservative who brought the flames of American wisdom to a world of folly.
Her intention was to bring the torch of freedom from America to the rest of the world.
The torch is not the path that illuminates the United States.”
It’s unusual that Limbaugh was actually moved by something.
The significance of the statue depends entirely on the perspective of cognition.
The most imaginative approach of Lazarus was to be able to see it. When the weary, wretched but hopeful people would be able to see it on the ship's deck after a long and often terrible journey.
Ostensibly, this involves the perspective of those who seek asylum and have the opportunity to improve their lives.
Her statue conveys to them and needs the hope and welcome of what they hear.
Of course, that’s not the point Miller and Trump once wanted to acknowledge.
Their torch was not to light the road, but to ignite the hatred of their sole source of strength.
