World Championships 2017: Neil Black praises Scottish players in the UK national team
Eilidh Doyle reminisced that there were only two Scots in the English national team at that time.
Neil Black, executive director of the British Athletics Association, said a team of 16 strong Scots in the World Championships group would make a "significant contribution to the national team".
The number of Scottish field athletes selected for the 2017 London Athletics World Championships, which opened on Friday, was the highest in history.
Black believes that "Scotland is making extraordinary progress" and British track and field athletes must learn from it.
“We accept and manage to understand that, and we’re going to hold on until we’re successful,” Black said.
"I'm going to talk to people at the Scottish Sports Institute. They think it's something to do with what they're doing.
Scottish track and field athletes believe it has something to do with their actions.
The British Athletics Association thinks it has something to do with their actions.
Even street people think so.
No matter what the situation, it's extremely brilliant."
A number of Scottish athletes, including Laura Muir and Andrew Butchart, will compete with Mo Farah in the 5000m, while Eilidh Doyle will be selected as the team’s captain.
“Irid is a little humble.
“It’s incredible that her captain’s speech,” Blake said.
“A lot of track and field athletes, regardless of whether they’re a Scot or wherever they’re living or training, are proud to walk out of the house.
It's a very good passion and real feeling that Eyred naturally put into it."
It is “incredible” that the Commonwealth silver medalist, who will compete in the 400-meter hurdles at its fifth world championship, said there were 16 Scots in the British national team.
Previously, the maximum number of Scottish track and field athletes participating in the World Series was seven, that is, the number of participants won in 1983 and 2015.
Müüüül is the leader. In the past year, she has set five UK records and two European records.
She will take part in the 1500-meter and 5000-meter competitions.
Chris O’Hare, who competes in the 1500m event, is another notable Scot, having previously achieved good results during the season, winning the British national qualifiers and the Olympic anniversaries, respectively.
Doyle remembers her participation in the first World Championship, when the only other Scot in the team was Lee McConnell.
The captain said that she is very proud that so many of her compatriots will not only join the team, but in some cases will also challenge medals to reach the final.
“What’s so special about being a national team captain is that it’s been voted on by the rest of the team. So it’s a great honor to know that your team-mates voted for something very unusual to happen to you, in any case for me,” she added.
“Of course, I’m very proud of all the Scottish players.
It’s just an indication of how far we’ve come.
Moreover, they are old track and field athletes here, track and field athletes who are about to advance to the goal of winning the podium.
How did my captain speak?
Well, I think it's great.
“I just refer to the fact that, in this case, we are very lucky because we held the world championships at home.
That’s why it’s really important for us to grow with our support at home and by getting back to the Olympic Stadium.
“I’m going to get a second chance to participate in the competition, and the atmosphere is going to be fantastic.”
