Block critics on Facebook: If you can't stand the censors of voters, don't run for public office
Our father-in-Charge is well-versed in the style of bad media.
American people loved Washington, but it wasn’t long before the newspapers lashed out at him for everything from domestic policy to political principles.
He’s irritated by these criticisms, no doubt about it.
But he didn’t acquiesce to these critics.
As early as 1783, Washington once said, “Freedom of speech will be denied – we will be slaughtered in silence and ignorance like a lamb.”
It made me think of Maryland’s governor, Larry Hogan, who needs to learn from Washington.
Hogan’s staff blocked and removed at least 450 people’s posts as they published their views on his official Facebook page.
The American Civil Liberties Union filed a lawsuit against him earlier this week.
The gubernator’s staff thought the lawsuit was sloppy, with online reports rife with a backlash: “Who cares?”
A lot of people say, “It’s just Facebook.”
However, this is very important.
It’s especially important because it’s someone like Hogan.
It’s a Republican who serves as governor in a Democratic-majority state, and he’s surprisingly popular.
Of the country's 50 governors, he received the 2nd acclaim.
Hogan was not a reactionary impetuous.
He was in control of Maryland and strictly adhered to the rules.
He is also very good at using Facebook as the main way to connect with constituents, teasing his bald head after chemotherapy on the home page for the first time.
So it's not necessary to block people who come to the gubernator's home page, and it's ultimately very dangerous. It's a public forum that's an official governing platform that's billed by public-paying employees.
In an interview with The Washington Post, Hogan’s spokeswoman Amelia Shas defended the governor’s actions, saying she thought blocking their comments was nothing more than trying to get them to exercise restraint.
But they too hastily described it as a flood beast or cynic, sweeping the web.
The Washington Post had spoken face-to-face with people who had been shielded by Hogan.
They’re just ordinary people talking to their chosen leaders: they’re teachers, small business owners, and pastors, they aren’t buffs.
They have said that their comments have depth and respect, and they are not indeprecated.
The pastor quoted the Bible in his post, calling on Hogan to remember his Catholic faith.
Rakshemi Salma Ramani, a barrister in Bowie, Maryland, was not cynical, but she asked questions about hate crimes.
“I commented politely that I was disappointed that he did not respond to hate crimes and other recent news events.
I also don’t like a bunch of people commenting on the same topic, because these people will be directly ignored by those who think that they are so-called herd mentality, rather than seeing them as a group of worried citizens.”
What the governor’s staff called the “intensity bombardment of junk information” may appear to others to be “insistance claims.”
The era of Facebook has made it easy to change a piece of information by shielding a critic or removing a negative comment.
It’s also much cleaner than in the old days, and to have the same effect in the past, it’s necessary to send staff out to collect and burn critical newspapers or arrest or silencourage the protesters.
But that’s really what’s happening right now, it’s just on a digital level.
Hogan isn’t the first public official to be accused of acquitting others on Facebook and other social media.
“President Trump is being sued by Twitter users for blocking them on Twitter.
A landmark ruling emerged in Virginia last week on such issues.
According to District Judge James C. Cataris of the U.S. City of Alexandria, the Lauden County Oversight Committee’s blocking of voters from her Facebook page violates the First Amendment.
Kentucky Gov. Matt Bevin (R) is also patronized by the U.S. Civil Liberties Union for its use of Facebook and Twitter.
It shouldn’t be so difficult.
In the age of Washington, affordable postage and the internet had a similar influence.
The number of newspapers quadrupled between 1776 and 1,800, with anonymous letters fiercely criticizing his leadership.
Even at that time, Washington had an anonymous flood beast against him.
People used pseudonyms such as Eurekla, Valerius, Belisearius, and Poius to severely criticize Washington's decision-making.
According to historical documents collected by the George Washington newspaper online project, his office was inundated with a petition, all criticizing his approach to the Treaty of Friendship, Commerce, and Navigation between His Majesty the King of England and the United States of America.
But he didn’t silence them.
“Freedom of speech, freedom of dissent and freedom of speech are the foundation of this country.
True leaders need to accept this.
