Ronan Rafferty looking forward to enjoying the feeling of going home at the Renaissance club
The East Lothian Revival Club will host the 25th Scottish Evergreen Open, and former European champion Ronan Rafferty will enjoy the feeling of returning home for the next three days.
The Northern Irishman led next-door Atcherfield Lynx to the final last year, but eventually lost to Paul Ills. He is now working for the new venue, representing the club's founder and CEO Jerry Salvati as tournament ambassador.
He said: “I’ve witnessed the establishment of the Renaissance club and we’re going to witness its glory through this competition.
Athletes will be able to see the best looks and the best shape of the stadium.
Jerry built this place unrivalled.
He’s a passionate advocate of this competition and is very proud of what he has achieved here.
It's the best place to show this course."
Ills of England is looking forward to defending for the first time, although in a 32-year-old career, he has won the European Tour and the European Challenge Tour at the same time.
The Southport athlete said: “It’s really special.
“I didn’t defend the Extremadula Open, because in the second year of my victory, the tournament was cancelled.
It feels amazing to come to this place.
It’s wonderful to play golf here.
Last year’s reminiscences surfaced and, for me and my wife Sharon, last year’s win had a special meaning for us.
It's really a special memory that we'll always remember."
Returning to his native competition was Gary Orr, who turned 50 earlier this year and then also participated in the European Evergreen Tour.
This is the first time he has competed in Scotland since the Scottish Open in 2013.
Helensburg Orr said: "There will always be more meaning to go back to the game.
You want to do it well, but you can feel the extra pressure.
To be able to play again, I’ve been playing golf.
I'm very happy with the current situation."
Joined by former Masters winner Ian Woucnan and Everathgreen winners Paul Brodhurst, Roger Chappmann, Mark James, and Mark McNewit.
A total of six Scottish Athletes on the field, Orr and former Ryder Cup captain Sam Torrance are the other two, and the other four are Andrew Audcoin, Stephen McCullister, Bill Langmu and Ross Drummond.
