Poverty and Dying in Jinzhou, Indonesia
Buddina Dejay doesn’t use the stove when she cooks.
She rarely even uses pots.
Her wooden house is located in Ena Rotali, the capital of Panai County in Indonesia’s Papua province, and the housewife usually puts just a sweet potato, which the locals call “nota” – directly into the fire.
After half an hour, this charred sweet potato was quickly taken out by a pair of unwashed hands and engulfed.
Dai Jie is sitting on the mud - she has no furniture, it is also a place for her to do daily household chores, such as washing clothes, the washing of clothes is very turbid, all from the nearby marshes.
There is a bucket in a room without a roof, which is the outdoor toilet.
She’s the youngest of her husband’s four wives, so she wasn’t taken care of.
Polygamy is very common here.
Of course, it is very dangerous to work until the party: most of the men in the village do not have a job, and a lot of them are drunk, and there are a lot of soldiers here.
She said, “No one dares to walk around the village after 5 p.m.”
This is a rare glimpse into everyday life in the Papuan Highlands, once a Dutch colony that was incorporated into Indonesia after a referendum in 1969, but was also controversial when only 1,026 elders were forced to vote by show of hands in front of the army that occupied the area.
An outspoken campaign against Dutch rule soon took aim at the Jakarta government, which imposes heavy-handed rule on the region and isolates foreign journalists and human rights defenders.
In 2003, the province was officially divided into the provinces of Papua and West Papua, while Papua New Guinea independently took control of the eastern part of the island.
Enarotali is a remote and uninhabited location; it is necessary to take a 90-minute flight from the provincial capital of Jayapura to Nabiray, and then rent a car for 5 hours, all the way to the stomach.
There is no public transport here.
In this town of about 19,000 people, wooden houses are surrounded by bamboo fencing, and folded tin roofs are all kinds of tawny-brown coverings made of rust.
There are very few Indonesian arrivals, let alone journalists, and almost no foreigners.
Prior to the arrival of Christian evangelists, the Mee Pago Papuans believed in a god called Uga Tamee.
There are other changes here too.
"We don't get used to wearing these clothes." She was referring to her bright-coloured turbans, dark shirts, and bright skirts.
“We used to cover our bodies with only leaves.”
Papua is one of Indonesia’s poorest provinces, with 28% of the population living below the poverty line and the region with the highest infant mortality rate and the lowest literate literate rate.
It’s also Indonesia’s Golden Continent.
Panai County is located about the same area as New Jersey, with 153,000 people. The world's largest and most profitable gold mine, Glasberg, is just 60 miles away and is owned by the Phoenix-based Felipe McMoran Copper & Gold Company.
In 2015 alone, Phillip mined about $3.1 billion worth of gold and copper.
In addition, Papua is rich in timber resources, with an estimated value of $78 billion.
However, these rich resources are also a source of tragedy for the Papuans, and Indonesia's powerful military forces have been able to suppress them.
According to a 2005 New York Times investigation, Phillip paid nearly $20 million to local military personnel and troops between 1998 and 2004, and an official alone was as high as $150,000.
The growing demands of the Papuans for their independence, which threatened the owner, was subjected to cruel treatment.
Since 1969, more than half a million Papuans have been killed and thousands have been raped, tortured and detained by Indonesian forces, according to activists.
According to the Asian Human Rights Commission, mass killings in the Papua tribal highlands in the 1970s escalated into genocidal.
Last year alone, Indonesian police arrested more than 3,900 peaceful protesters.
A 2016 report by the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Brisbane, “We’ll Lose Everything,” includes proofs of the previous year’s horrendous actions, such as extrajudicial killings, tortures – another report that says rapes and electricity are particularly prevalent – as well as a brutal crackdown on peaceful demonstrations.
Andreas Halsono, a researcher at Human Rights Watch in Indonesia, said: “Violence occurs on a daily basis, making it difficult to calculate the number of victims.”
The resources of Papua attracted a large number of immigrants from outside Indonesia, and the oppression began.
83 per cent of the province's 3.5 million people are Christians, but the demographics of Muslim economic migrants have changed since the densely populated islands of Java, Kalimantan, Sumatra, and Sulawesi arrived.
Javanese food stalls sell fried chicks and veggies mixed with peanut butter.
The locals are struggling.
Abos You, a 24-year-old Panai native, who moved to the provincial capital Chayapura to look for a job, explained that “the immigrants started selling chicken and vegetables in traditional markets at a lower price than local Papuans.”
“This caused the local Papuans—mama-mama women—to lose their markets.”
Indonesian President Joko Widodo (often referred to as Joko Widodo) vowed at his 2014 election campaign to address Papua’s inequality and abuse of power.
The former carpenter’s path to Jakarta’s presidential palace is supported in 27 of all 29 regions – including Panai.
But nothing has changed in Papua, and today the locals feel betrayed.
“We are already heartbroken that in 2014 we voted for Jokowi, in the hope that he will fulfill his wish and restore fairness.”
In fact, just two months after Jokowi took office in October, Panai suffered his worst nightmare.
On December 7, 2014, 11 children in Erna Rotali sang a Christmas carol in front of an outside bonfire when two Indonesian soldiers rushed over on a motorbike.
The frightened children were told that they should turn on the headlights.
One of the soldiers was disgruntled with their tone, according to the local pastor, and then came back with the four soldiers.
The victims and witnesses said the drunken soldiers chased the children with their guns.
Then one of the soldiers fired a shot at the children.
Sixteen-year-old Julianus Yemer was in a coma after being badly beaten.
A few hours later, a blaze ignited the building of the Election Commission of a nearby government agency, escalated the incident the next day.
More than 1,000 young Papuan men, women and children gathered at a football stadium in front of the local police station and the military command centre to demand justice.
With ceremonial hunting bows, they danced the waita dance of the Babua Mee Pago tribe—running in circles and imitiating birdsong.
Some of the protesters began throwing stones at regular military outposts.
According to an official document seen by Time magazine, when the mood continued to escalate, the soldiers received the order via an internal radio: "If the crowd resists more than three times, shoot them immediately." However, the document was not released to the local media.
The 56-year-old Jeremias Kayamu, the leader of the Kego Koto neighbourhood in Ana Rotali, sensed that danger was imminent and called on the crowd to calm down and implored them to return home.
But no one was in the mood to listen to him.
As he stood on the porch of his bright-colored wooden house, he told Time magazine, "When I looked back, my left wrist was suddenly shot."
Cayamu still doesn’t yet know who fired the gun, but he said the bullets came from among the assembled soldiers.
He also said, "It was very crowded, they fired a lot of shots."
According to the cousins of Orvyus Juul, he witnessed the shooting and saw Orfyus Juul being shot three times.
Yuhanes grievably told Time magazine, “I ran over to check his body to see if it was him.
“I found out he’s dead, and I’ve kissed him.”
Papua police superintendent Yuji Mende told reporters that his subordinates were only "protecting" the police station because the police station had been attacked.
In 2015, Papua police spokesman and police chief Puezo Sulistio said: "When someone threatens to kill us, we have to defend ourselves."
It's a matter of death."
According to Human Rights Watch, five young protesters were killed and many more were injured.
The next day, news of the killings only spread slowly in Jakarta.
Three weeks later, Jokowi made a speech in Jayapura, expressing deep sympathy to the families of the victims and vowing to resolve the abuses left over from Papua’s history.
“I hope that this case will be resolved as soon as possible, so that it will not happen again in the future.”
In October 2016, Security Minister Verando said he wanted to set up a non-judicial mechanism to address the historical legacy of human rights violations.
However, the excuse of disengagement also almost immediately sounded.
He said, “Most of the violations happened a long time ago.
Some were in the 1990s and early 2000s.
The point is that we are already committed to addressing these violations, but it still requires a process.”
Then in Jakarta on June 5 in a conversation with Time, Wilando said that he had no plans to establish a complaints mechanism in Papua.
On the contrary, he said that “everything will be done in accordance with the law”.
The U.N. has filed a complaint against Willando for more than 1,000 deaths in a bloody 1999 independence vote in East Timor over “crimes against human rights,” but he says 11 human rights violations have been resolved in the Papua region, including the Panai incident.
The families of the victims of the Panai incident scoffed at these remarks.
“I’ve had four interviews in the last three years, but things aren’t progressing at all.”
Many years later he said, "I'm tired."
He is still living in a state of fear.
He said, “I’m scared.
“I’m afraid of being arrested by the military, I’m afraid of being shot.”
His brother Yukabus also believed that the people of Panai were afraid to talk about the incident.
He said he was beaten by the military after helping to bury the four victims.
“The military came looking for me after burying the body.”
The shooting didn’t stop.
On Tuesday, Indonesian police gunned down villagers in De Yai County, Parnair district.
The manager of a construction company refused to help transport a comatose man to the hospital, clashing between the two sides, killing one and wounding 17 others, including children.
Ramayanus Dowu, a 24-year-old man who drowned while fishing in a nearby river, died on his way to the hospital.
Angry villagers protested in front of the company’s on-site office.
The police said the villagers threw stones at the police, so the police fired a warning.
However, riot mobile units (Indonesian paramilitary police) began shooting at the crowd, and one person was killed.
The 29-year-old Domingu Buddy told Time magazine that “we were panicked at the time and are now afraid that they will retaliate.” He was living near the hospital, where he witnessed the wounded being rushed to the hospital.
“I’ve been at home for two days.”
The De Yai parliament has called for the police involved to be brought to justice and riot mobile troops to be removed from the area.
Panai has always been a rightful place for the Indonesian government.
The lack of effective development fosters the grievances of the Mee, Moni, Dani, and Damal tribes, which are scattered in the lush central highlands of Phani.
A large number have joined the Free Papua Movement (OPM), a rebel force that claims to protect the rights of the Papuans through scattered raids and raids to abduct Indonesian soldiers.
The top leaders of the Liberal Papuan movement were born in Panai, including Tadius Euge and Daniel Yudas Kogoya.
As a result, in recent years, thousands of panai people have been arrested by the military under the guise of “maintaining national sovereignty” and deliberately detained.
Some did not show up again.
In the eyes of the baboons, Panai is a “forgotten land of tragedy.”
Poverty breeds displeasure.
Enarotali sells so little rice that the locals simply can’t afford them.
Bread is unattainable.
The people here grow everything they can eat: mostly sweet potatoes and some fruits and leafy vegetables.
Cultivation is a woman's job, and every woman is able to take care of four or five sweet potato fields.
They usually keep most of the harvest for writers, and the rest is sold in the local market.
Ten pieces of sweet potatoes are only worth 10,000 Indonesian rupees (75 cents).
Over time, economic inequities have spread among local Papuans and newcomers, and the number of such migrants has increased significantly since the opening of a new route to the Nabiray airport.
A small number of existing jobs often fall into the hands of better-educated and wealthier immigrants.
Papuans have very little funding or the necessary skills to run a competitive company.
John Goby, president of the Parnair Tribal Council, said that “young people don’t want to stay in the village ... because there are no jobs and no money”.
Papua was isolated from the world.
In addition, the restrictions on international media coverage are still very tight.
Earlier this year, French journalists Frank Escudi and Basel Longchamp were expelled from Papua because they “don’t cooperate with the relevant agencies,” even though they’ve rarely been licensed for filming.
Fulim Kane, deputy Asia director at Human Rights at Human Rights Watch, said Jokowi’s election pledge to withdraw reporting restrictions to promote transparency and development had not materialized.
He said in a statement that “if foreign journalists are trying to report on Papua and West Papua in Indonesia’s easternmost disgruntled provinces, they run a new risk: denial of visas and blacklisting.”
The lack of media supervision means that international pressure on the Indonesian government is largely limited to Papua’s neighbors.
In March, six Pacific nations – Tonga, Nauru, Palau, Tuvalu, the Marshall Islands and the Solomon Islands – asked the UN Human Rights Council to investigate “a wide range of widespread human rights violations,” including the Panai shooting.
These countries have also supported the Free Papua Movement .
A spokesman for the Indonesian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Almanata Nasir, was unconcerned about the group’s allegations, telling reporters in Jakarta that “Indonesia continues to have democratic institutions in place, and the media is free, so the evidence of human rights violations is difficult to conceal.”
The local people hope that more foreign governments will pay attention.
According to a person familiar with the matter, when the Dutch ambassador for human rights, Keswan Barr, led a delegation of official officials during a visit to Chayapura on May 4, the local people broke their silence and begged "we want freedom." He also participated in the meeting, but requested anonymity.
Indonesia will hold the next presidential election in 2019, but the Papuans say they are unlikely to vote in Jokowi again.
Goby, the chairman of the tribal council, said: “Zokovi’s intentions were good, but he was surrounded by people who were involved in the Panai shooting.”
He wanted Jokowi to know that the people of Panai not only lived under the threat of a voracious army, but that there would be little education in the region and a lack of public health-care services.
Gobai said the people of Panai, like other Papuans, believed that the vote they voted for Jokowi was a debt he would have to repay.
“They don’t need money, they need justice.”
Even under threat and intimidation, the families of the victims of the Panai shooting made their last symbolic revolt: burying the body of one of the victims in front of the military police post.
They know that justice may never come, but at least they won’t let the culprits forget their crimes.
Ekabus lowered his head and said, “My family has been killed.
“What else can we do?”
On February 10, 2017, the China Securities Regulatory Commission approved the initial application of the Guangdong Shangpin Home Furnishing Co., Ltd., and launched a small and medium-to-small board listing pass on the Shenzhen Stock Exchange, after more than 20 days, the first IPO was approved.
Industry insiders believe that with the subsequent listing of Shangpin's home furnishing and European homes, the field of customized homes that was originally on the tuyere will shift from product competition to capital competition.
At the beginning of the new New Year, the custom home is a typical representative of the news of the upcoming listing of the brand in the field, attracting the attention of the entire home furnishing industry.
According to Reuters on the 9th, Samsung Electronics on Tuesday released the first self-driving car components jointly developed with Harman International, as well as infotainment solutions, which is an important measure for the company to enter the vehicle electronics market.
Samsung Electronics acquired Harman International about a year ago.
At this week's Consumer Electronics Show (CES) in Las Vegas, Samsung unveiled a car camera with crash warnings, lane offset warnings, and cruise control features, developed in partnership with Harman.
Samsung plans to start shipping the camera system this year, which is based on technologies such as automatic machine learning and augmented reality.
Samsung has also demonstrated a “digital cockpit” that allows the vehicle to be more personalized even if the driver is not the owner, because the system allows users to download the driver’s data from mobile devices and the cloud.
