The fire engulfed the "flame" tower in Dubai once again.
A fire broke out in one of the world’s tallest residential buildings in Dubai on Friday, forcing hundreds of residents to flee the 3-37-meter-tall, 79-story supertall.
This is the second blaze in just two years to sweep high-rise buildings, which happens to happen to take place in a tower known as the Torch.
Evacuations have been made to the building and there have been no reports of injuries, and the cause of the blaze has not yet been identified.
“We were going to sleep and then we were woken up by the fire and people started screaming.
“It takes about 10 minutes for us to run down the stairs from the 50th floor,” a resident named George told Reuters.
Dubai's civil protection authority said the fire was extinguished at about 4 a.m. local time (0000 GMT) and was cooling the building.
The government said it was making efforts to provide a place of refuge for those affected.
The accident could once again raise concerns around the world about the safety of high-rise buildings using façade materials.
An investigation carried out by the management of the "Vilnam" skyscraper in the aftermath of the fire in 2015 found that most of the damaged objects were cladding façades for decoration and insulation.
British police have said they believe problems with the insulation and cladding system at the Grenfell Tower in London may have contributed to the rapid spread of the fire in June, which killed about 80 people.
Williams has also hinted that Massa is likely to return, even though he has already announced his retirement.
On January 9, only a month away from the opening of the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics in South Korea, the atmosphere of the Winter Olympic Games in South Korea has become more and more intense.
South Korean public opinion expects that this long-lost inter-Korean talks will bring an “egg” a month after the countdown to the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics.
On November 1st of last year, the Pyeongchang Winter Olympic flame arrived in South Korea.
It is directly reflected in ticket sales for the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics.
Just more than two months ago, the ticket sales rate of the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics was only hovering at 32%, and the ticket sales rate of the Winter Paralympics was only 4.3%, and the sluggish ticket sales rate once made the outside world worried about the popularity of the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics.
However, as Li Xifan, chairman of the organizing committee of the Pyeongchang Winter Olympic Games, predicted to reporters not long ago that "as the flame of the Winter Olympic Games is transmitted in various parts of South Korea, the popularity of the Olympic Winter Games is increasing, and I believe that the ticket sales situation will soon improve."
According to South Korean media reports, as of January 3, ticket sales for the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics have reached 64%, and the ticket sales rate for the Winter Paralympics has also exceeded 50%.
In order to encourage foreigners to go to South Korea to watch the competition, the South Korean Ministry of Justice also announced on the 8th that it will extend the stay in South Korea for foreigners who watch the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics to stay in South Korea for a maximum of 120 days.
More than one in five U.S. travelers bring prohibited items on board.
A survey of aircraft passengers found that more than 20 percent of passengers had intentionally or unintentionally encased illegal items to board through U.S. Transportation Security Administration checkpoints, with at least 6 percent of passengers accidentally carrying prohibited knives or other edges.
Fewer than 1 percent of the passengers said they only later found out they were traveling with a firearm, ammunition, or explosives.
The survey of more than 1,000 people was conducted by charter services company Stratos Jet Charters Inc., which also found that young travelers are more likely to blatantly ignore regulations.
19.7 per cent of the respondents who admitted to deliberately bringing TSA-prohibited items on board were Millennials, compared with less than 15 per cent of Generation Xers.
One of the most common items that deliberately slips through TSA is food and beverages.
More than 3 per cent admitted to knowingly carrying a blade through security, while 2.2 per cent of female respondents and 3.7 per cent of male respondents said they had knowingly carried illegal drugs on the plane.
The company said that the survey of national travelers is to understand the number of people who accidentally carried and deliberately carried embargoed goods through the security inspection of the aircraft.
It’s not surprising that the people who are most likely to carry embargoed goods through security checks—intentionally or unintentionally—are those who fly most frequently.
However, the company points out that the TSA guidelines are a bit complicated and incomprehensible.
It’s often unclear what kind of fluid you can carry.
Therefore, it is not surprising that TSA seizes exactly the most prohibited liquids.
At one point, it was thought that the TSA might also treat books as an embargo.
Domestic flights are allowed to carry liquids, but international flights are not allowed.
U.S. Environmental Protection Agency Administrator Prudt changes his mind on Obama-era emissions-reduction regulations
A day after being sued in 15 states, Environmental Protection Agency Administrator Scott Pruitt reversed his earlier decision to delay the implementation of Obama-era regulations to reduce emissions of smoky air pollutants.
Pruitt proposed the change, because the Environmental Protection Agency was able to respond more quickly to the needs of environmental regulatory state institutions than previous governments.
He made no mention of a legal challenge raised by a federal appeals court against his former post.
Controversy centers on setting an Oct. 1 deadline for states to start meeting 2015 ground-level ozone standards.
In June, Pruitt announced that he would be delaying compliance by a year in order to give the Environmental Protection Agency more time to study the plan and avoid “disrupting local decision-making or hindering economic growth.”
Prior to his appointment by President Donald Trump, Pruitt was Oklahoma’s state attorney general, a long-standing opposition to the rigor of environmental regulations.
Since arriving in Washington, Prudt has repeatedly switched to blocking or postponing regulations opposed by the chemical and fossil fuel industries.
Wednesday’s abrupt inversions are the latest legal obstacle to Prudential’s return to the agenda.
Last month, a U.S. federal appeals court in Washington ruled that the Environmental Protection Agency overstepped its authority in an attempt to delay the enforcement of the Obama administration’s rules requiring oil and gas companies to monitor and reduce methane leaks.
In a statement released on Wednesday night, Prudt offered a U-turn on his approach to ozone standards, saying he wanted to strengthen the Environmental Protection Agency’s commitment to work with states and adopt complex procedures to reach new standards.
Pruitt said that "under the previous government, the EPA usually did not meet the specified deadline, and then waited for radical groups and other people to sue, and then agreed to set a designated timetable through an agreement."
“We don’t believe in regulation through litigation and we’re serious about deadlines.
We also take the regulations and the authorities very seriously.”
Nevertheless, the EPA’s statement said Pruitt may again use his “extended authorization and all other legally available authorizations” to ensure that the regulations comply with “good policy and the best information available.”
Republicans in Congress are campaigning for a wide-ranging rewriting of ozone layer regulations.
The House bill, approved last month, aims to delay the 2015 rule by at least eight years.
The measure has not yet been put to a vote in the Senate.
New York Attorney General Eric Schneederman led a confederate of states in suing the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency this week, saying it intends to maintain legal pressure.
“The Environmental Protection Agency’s inversion — after we’ve litigated the lawsuit — was a significant victory for the health and safety of 6.7 million New Yorkers, with communities of more than 115 million Americans directly affected by smog emissions.”
California, Connecticut, Delaware, Illinois, Iowa, Maine, Massachusetts, Minnesota, New Mexico, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, Vermont, and the U.S. District of Columbia all joined New York in the case.
Ground-level ozone is caused by common pollutants emitted by automobiles, power plants, refineries, chemical plants and other sources that react under sunlight in the atmosphere.
The resulting smog can cause serious respiratory problems for sensitive people, causing thousands of premature deaths every year.
Of course, public-health advocates and environmentalists hailed Pruitt’s surprising transformation.
Lori Ann Bird of the Centre for Biological Diversity said: “It’s unsettling that this common-sense measure comes under intense pressure from the head of the department that protects the air we need to breathe.”
“We must fight to the end with the Trump administration’s ideological campaign to cater to polluters and special interests.”
