Moore Beach Tragedy: Activists Resume Racist lynchings for Justice
A 1948-year-on-a-year-old-year-old Ford Mercury rides through a group of onlookers in the small town of Menroe, Georgia, roosting toward the tiny Moor Beach Bridge.
There were four black passengers and a white driver in the car, and before the car arrived at the bridge, a group of white people with guns marched forward and blocked the car's way forward.
The thug’s leader – a middle-aged man in a pinstriped suit – took a deep sip of a cigar and glanced into the windshield.
The passengers in the car were full of fear.
“What we want – Roger!” the man shouted.
“Get him out of the car!”
A young negro was dragged down from the front passenger seat with screams piercing the silence.
Fortunately, this terrible scene on the 22nd of July is just a repeat.
However, when "Roger" put a noose around his neck, almost all the spectators made a real respite.
Since 2005, civil rights Activists have returned to Moore Beach Bridge, renouncing two black couples – Roger and Dorothy Malkham, as well as George and Mere Dorsi – who were lynched in 1946 by the Three K’s parties on the night of their lynchings.
No one has ever been charged with murder.
“While we know who they are, even though we’ve seen of them in the community, it’s incredible that none of them have been arrested over the years,” said Tyrone Brooks, who helped organize the re-enactment.
“This is the stain of history and a burden on our souls.”
Brooks, 71, is a former Georgia congressman and lifelong human rights activist.
For him, the reenactment was a compelling call to remind the people of the Monroe community every year that injustice had never been corrected.
“We’re going to prosecute, we’re going to close the case, we’re going to heal, we want reconciliation, but we’re going to get justice first,” he said.
“Unless we receive justice, there will be no healing and reconciliation.”
On July 27, 1946, a coroner placed a sheet on the body of a Moor Beach lynching victim.
According to FBI files obtained by NBC News, more than 50 men from Monroe and neighboring counties are suspected of lynching.
But none of them have been charged.
While the suspects have been at large for decades, civil rights activists say the dual motive for lynchings is well known throughout the town: politics and sex.
In April 1946, a U.S. Supreme Court decision enabled black citizens of Georgia to vote in primaries for the first time later in the summer of that year.
At about the same time, Roger Malcolm, a black bunker, stabbed the white landowner Barnet Hirst in a fight — as if it were for a woman, according to FBI statistics.
Brooks said there were rumors in the town that Hirst slept with Malcolm’s wife, Dorothy, and that her baby wasn’t of her husband’s.
On July 25, 1946, prominent white landowner Roy Harrison bailed Malcolm out of prison for $600.
Dorothea and her cousins, Michael Murray and George Dorsey, accompanied him to prison.
However, when they came back from prison and were nearing the Moor Beach Bridge, a group of lynching rioters surrounded the car.
“It turns out that it was Harrison who organized and ordered this murder,” an FBI report concluded.
“It’s clear that there was a conspiracy between state and local law enforcement who not only participated in the murder, destroyed the evidence, but also concealed the identities of the witnesses.”
Bryce was very brief.
“It’s a vote tragedy,” he said.
“They were brutally murdered, and it was the murderer who sent a message to the black people in this community: ‘If you register and vote, you’re going to have the same outcome.”
For nearly a decade, Cassandra Green has been in charge of an annual re-enactment of the Moore Beach Bridge lynching case.
It's figurative, heart-wrenching, with a deeply racist background. Many hope that this kind of racism no longer exists in the United States.
It’s hard to see, it’s harder not to look, but Green says that’s the key.
“We don’t want to talk about lynchings because lynching puts us on the ugly side of everyone,” she said.
“But if you don’t listen to me, or if you don’t hear about my experiences, I can’t have a relationship with you.”
This year’s show began in June, with nearly 30 actors — more than a dozen whites and the rest black — huddled in a room to listen to Green’s rehearsed-out performance.
Wade Malbow served as Green’s co-director.
For many years, he has played the role of the Ku Klux Klan, but he said that the role has never been easy.
“I felt dirty because I wasn’t that kind of person,” Malbau said.
“But I think it’s very important to keep this history alive, because we don’t want to go back to that era.”
In the room, actors who played Roger Malcolm and Barnet Hirst Darios Bradshaw and Nick Lascona were rehearsing their lines.
At the beginning of the play, Malcolm yells and pushes away Hirst, indignant that he sleeps with his wife, Dorothy.
“I said, get out of my place!”
Lascona fought back.
And then they fought.
Then everyone (or at least all the black actors) burst into laughter.
Green laughed the most.
“What’s the matter?” asked Lascona.
He was panicked and confused.
Have I played too far?
“No,” Green said with a laugh.
“I just don’t think white people don’t say, ‘Ah,’ when they hit back.”
“Yes,” quipped another black actor.
“Your tone has to be tough.”
In this way, the energy in the room has changed; the feeling of fraternity drives away the feeling of discomfort.
Everyone is trying to do this, not only to perform the play, but also to commemorate the four victims.
The scene begins again.
This time, when Raskona said the "ah" word, the tone was sharp and powerful.
There’s no one laughing.
The re-enactment ended powerfully and quickly.
Screams, gunshots, and then there was silence.
A woman, dressed in black at the funeral, was standing next to the actor’s body, singing Billy Halede’s words of condolence “strange fruit.”
Her voice pressed through other words.
More than a hundred yards, about forty spectators were wiping sweat and tears.
Blacks and whites accounted for almost half of the audience.
There was a couple who made a special trip from Ithaca, New York, and there were a lot of people who lived near the road.
Brooks took them to the venerations of Malcolm and Dorothy for nearly eight hours, and everyone was suffering from Georgia’s uncomfortable turbulence.
Until this time, the murderer was still a fascination, but now, standing in the same land where the lynching took place, there was anxiety in the air.
When the song’s final note disappears, Green asks people to come closer and examine the bodies played by the actors.
The children leaned down and looked at the fake blood and the bodies, whispering to each other.
“They aren’t really dead,” a little boy muttered, as if reminding himself.
After a few minutes, Green thanked the crowd, then the show ended.
The actor throws off the lynching rope and wipes away the tears.
Bradshaw hugged Malbau, the man in the suit who played the head of the Three K's.
Nick Rasconer hugged two of his colleagues, weeping loudly in their arms; the emotions in the performance eventually infected him.
“It doesn’t matter,” one of the women whispered.
“We know that’s not the case in your heart.
There's a reason you're doing that."
The audience then sat down for dinner with the actors.
At the same table, a white couple from Atlanta chatted intimately with a black couple they had just met to talk about a reenactment.
Denis Duprinski had a hard time finding words to describe how she felt after watching.
“It’s hard to imagine those horrible words and actions ... people like you say and do, because they look very similar to you,” she said.
“To me, it’s really made me think the lynchings are still going on,” she added. “Right now it’s called a police shooting.”
In the room, Tyrone Brooks sat down at the table to watch, savoring and regurgitating civil rights.
He says he’s already working on next year’s show.
“It’s an inspiring way to repeat because it describes the horrors we’ve experienced, but it’s also very instructive,” he said.
“If you don’t learn from it, it’ll probably happen again.
Those who don't study and don't understand their own history are doomed to repeat it."
