Chinese Air Force Engineering University scientists study laser satellite used to remove space junk
U.S. scientists have put forward a bold and innovative proposal to use large-scale lasers to clean up the clutter of Earth’s orbit and destroy old satellites as well as its space junk.
According to the U.S. "Newsweek" website reported on January 15th, researchers at the Chinese Air Force Engineering University have written a paper entitled "Space-based laser removal of small-scale space debris reorbiting model research", which describes how through the use of space-based platform lasers, space debris can be irradiated by lasers into smaller, less harmful debris.
For many years, space junk has been a matter of concern to the international space agency, and NASA has considered a variety of options for tracking, detecting and removing man-made debris.
Ways to remove large-scale space debris include giant nets used to clean and collect waste, as well as magnets that can be used to suck them out of their orbits. Chinese scientists conducted laser simulation studies and concluded that this would be an effective way to clean up Earth's orbit.
The abstract of the paper states: “(simulation studies) provides the necessary theoretical basis for the application of space-based laser space debris removal techniques.”
A 2014 study by U.S. defense giant Lockheed Martin found that there were about 200 threats a day for satellites orbiting.
High-velocity debris moving along Earth’s orbit is seen as a serious threat to future space missions and has been described by European Space Agency experts as a “deadly cascade of threats.” High-speed debris has continued to increase over the past year.
On January 17, the U.S. "Volkswagen Machinery" monthly website reported that space is basically empty, but space next to Earth is becoming more and more congested. Every satellite we send into space will eventually turn into another piece of debris orbiting the earth, and these pieces will accumulate over time.
The more debris there is, the higher the chance that a satellite that is still in normal operation will be hit by the debris, thus damaging or destroying a multi-million-dollar investment.
According to the report, all space agencies have developed plans to remove space junk in the future.
NASA is considering de-orbiting the speed of the splray gas to de-orbit it, Europe is considering launching a satellite carrying a large net to capture the debris and bring it back to Earth, and the Japanese idea is to capture the debris with an electric chain.
At present, China has also proposed a solution to space junk, that is, to use a large laser to smash floating space junk into less harmful small debris.
According to a paper published by a team of researchers at the Chinese Air Force Engineering University in the German journal Optics, installing such a laser on a satellite would be efficient enough to clean up the space, at least according to the simulation.
According to the report, the only way to prove its effectiveness is to conduct the test, there are currently no arrangements for such a mission, but perhaps sooner or later someone will install a laser on a satellite and send it into space.
“Once that’s done, we can finally begin to eliminate the problem of space junk once and for all.”
(Compilation/Hu Jing, Wang Haixuan)
