Pearson lays off another 3,000 jobs and slashed its dividend to rejuvenate the business.
British education group Pearson said on Friday that it would lay off another 3,000 jobs and significantly cut its interim dividend by 72%, as it has embarked on another restructuring project to deal with business pressures.
Chief Executive Officer John Farlon said that most job cuts will take place in the second half of 2018 and the first half of 2019, following a reduction of 4000 positions in the previous restructuring.
Pearson announced in May that it planned to cut an additional £300 million ($394 million) in a third cost-cutting project to revive a business impacted by the rapid development of digital learning.
Last month, the company agreed to sell its stake in Penguin Random House to partner Bertelsmann and published a report saying its basic sales rose by 1% in the first half of the year to £2.45 billion, but said the outlook for this year remains the same.
