A suicide bombing of a convoy in Kabul has killed NATO soldiers.
According to official sources, a military convoy was attacked by suicide bombers in the Afghan capital Kabul, killing a NATO soldier and wounding several others.
A NATO statement said five soldiers and an interpreter were wounded.
Unconfirmed local reports indicate that three civilians were also killed in the incident in the city’s Karabagh district.
This is the latest violent attack in Afghanistan - Tuesday's Herat mosque bombing killed more than 30 people.
On Monday, a suicide bomber killed two people near an Iraqi embassy in Kabul.
According to the NATO statement, there was an explosion on Thursday night when a joint Afghan-NATO coalition patrol was hit by “individuals carrying improvised explosive devices.”
The statement said that all the injured are currently in stable condition and are being treated in a U.S. military hospital at Bagram Air Force Base.
According to reporters, the growing Taliban and the so-called Islamic State (IS) group in the Karabakh region have raised serious concerns among NATO forces stationed near Bagram.
According to United Nations statistics, at least 1,662 civilians were killed in Afghanistan in the first half of 2017, compared with about 20 percent in the capital.
At least 30 people were killed in a suicide car bombing in Kabul’s Shiite-dominated region in late July.
The Taliban claimed to have carried out the attack.
More than 150 people were killed in a huge explosion in the center of Kabul on May 31, the deadliest militant attack in the country since a U.S.-led multinational toppled the Taliban in 2001.
U.S. President Donald Trump is weighing whether the country’s perilous security situation comes at a time of increasing Afghan support for its military and police.
The suicide bombings in Kabul in recent weeks have also sparked a crisis of public confidence in the government and its policies, particularly President Ashraf Ghani.
U.S. U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) overturns resolution after lawsuit, delays smog regulations
At the time of Obama's tenure, he ordered states to curb various types of smog-causing emissions, and 15 states sued the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency for that rule. A day later, the EPA overturned the rule.
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) on Wednesday announced its decision to proceed with the so-called “O ozone-to-energy zone of 2015” and promised that it would work with the states.
U.S. EPA Administrator Scott Pruitt said in a statement: “We have full confidence in dialogue with our national partners and are committed to responding to it.”
Pruitt announced in June that the Environmental Protection Agency plans to postpone the implementation of ozone-to-standard zone standards until October 2018, with a one-year delay – according to which existing smog pollution can be measured in some areas to determine which areas should reduce emissions that cause smog pollution in order to meet air quality standards.
Fifteen major Democratic states, as well as the District of Columbia, filed a lawsuit on Tuesday claiming it was against the law.
As part of a broader agenda, President Donald Trump’s administration has struggled to reinstate a variety of environmental regulations pursued by former President Barack Obama to spur economic growth.
However, these efforts have sparked opposition from Democrats and conservative groups. They are concerned that more emissions will affect public health.
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency’s website states that breathing ozone-containing air “decreases lung function and increases respiratory symptoms,” such as asthma.
