Sebastián Ke praises Russia for 'frank' apology over doping scandal
On Thursday, Sebastián Ke, president of the International Athletics Federation, applauded Russia’s apology over the doping scandal, saying it was “frank”, but he reiterated that it would not be the right time for Russia to return to the sport.
The head of Russia’s athletics federation, Mitri Chiakdin, said at the IAAF conference on the eve of the World Athletics Championships that it was right for his country to be banned from the sport and that he was determined to combat doping.
He said he regretted that “all the athletes who had won gold and silver medals in the competition were deprived of their medals”.
Despite his comments, the IAAF Congress voted in favour of maintaining the ban, which was previously reported by the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA), which found evidence of Russian state-sponsored stimulant use, was implemented in November 2015.
“I think today’s response was a very candid, a very candid introduction,” IAAF President Frank told reporters.
“It is a great pleasure for the entire Council and the IAAF... The Russian Federation acknowledges that it has gone through a period of great burning and is doing everything possible to ensure that Russia can re- participate in the competition.”
Ke said that he was also "happy" that Russia accepted the standard of re-competition.
“I think it’s a very constructive day and I also think we’ve made some progress, but it’s not the right time for the IAAF to support the task force’s recommendation and it’s not the right time to reinvigorate Russia,” Koch said.
“We need to do everything we can in the next few months to normalize the situation.
“Our guiding principle has always been the desire to separate innocent athletes from the tainted system,” he added.
Ninety-nine Russian Athletes, in line with the IAAF’s strict standards, will compete as Neutral Competitors at the World Athletics Championships in London on Friday.
The head of the IAAF’s task force, Vhun Anderson, said on Monday that Russia had not yet met a number of criteria to resume participation.
Speaking to the IAAF council in London, he told reporters that drug testing was still insufficient and that banned coaches were still coaching.
