What is the impact of football’s financial rules on Neymar’s move to Paris?
Rules designed to prevent unfettered spending by big European football clubs do not prevent Neymar’s move from Spain’s FC Barcelona to France’s Paris Saint-Germain (PSG) at a record price.
As a result, the European Football Association (UEFA) further relegation of the Paris Saint-Germain Football Club. The European Football Federation is the governing body of European football and has the power to ban teams from the much-watched Champions League.
UEFA’s Financial Fair Play Rules (FFP), introduced in 2011, means that Paris Saint-Germain will eventually have to prove that Neymar’s transfer is funded without causing huge losses.
The Barcelona club has already paid 222 million euros ($262 million) just to pay for the Brazilian player’s release, and Neymar’s salary will be as high as tens of millions of dollars a year.
Here are some of Neymar’s financial implications for his move to France.
Bearing in mind that the team’s funding comes from a well-financed Qatar’s ruling family, Paris-Saint-Germain football club can certainly sign Neymar in cash and include him in other star-studded teams.
The club can spend a lot of money to buy players, but they have to use a legitimate source of income to make up for the huge amount of money they spend, so that they can achieve a break-even in their football-related businesses.
The Financial Fair Play Rules were conceived by Michel Platini after he was elected president of UEFA in 2007.
The former French player believes that for clubs that are heavily indebted in the pursuit of success, if the bank or everyone withdraws support funds, they are actually defrauding and putting the club at risk of destruction.
In 2011, the focus of the Rules shifted from the club’s debt problems to the club’s loss condition monitoring.
This change means that Roman Abramovich used the business model when he bought the Chelsea club in 2003, to buy a club with potential and then spend a lot of money to improve the team and build a global brand, is now facing a harsher situation.
The club is likely to lose 30 million euros during the three-year rule assessment period up to 2018.
Despite being denominated by Abu’s presidency in 2015, UEFA’s successor, Aleksander Ceferin, remains committed to stopping excessive spending.
“Of course the rules haven’t lapsed yet, and we’re definitely going to strengthen the regulations of the future,” Andrea Traverso of UEFA, who is in charge of rule-related matters, told The Associated Press last week.
Although Paris Saint-Germain will pay Neymar's buy-out clause in a one-time payment, it can also distribute the amount according to the purpose of accounting during the duration of the contract.
Neymar has already signed a five-year deal, so transfer fees are likely to show a fee of 44.4 million euros in annual accounts for the next five years.
The first wave of “Rules” sanctions imposed in 2014 resulted in the harshest sanctions on Paris-Saint-Germain Football Club, as well as the Abu Dhabi-owned Manchester City club.
Paris Saint-Germain was fined 60 million euros (US$82 million according to the current exchange rate), which was later reduced to 20 million euros, and requested that in the 2014-15 Champions League the team reduce the number of players to 21, rather than the normal 25 players.
UEFA is staring at clubs that are trying to increase revenue and defraud financial regulators by pegging the club’s owners.
While Qatar’s companies can sponsor Paris-Saint-Germain FC, Uefa will not treat it as a legitimate income if the sponsorship amount is believed to exceed the standard market price.
That’s something that happened in 2014, when UEFA ruled that the Qatar Tourism Authority’s sponsorship of Paris Saint-Germain’s money had been overstated.
A portion of Neymar’s revenue at Saint-Germain Football Club in Paris could reportedly come from payments made by the organizing committee for the 2022 World Cup in Qatar.
However, FC Saint-Germain in Paris and the organizing committee have denied that Neymar will serve as World Cup ambassador, and it will be easy for UEFA's regulators to make such a move.
The recent imposition of sanctions on Saint-Germain in Paris means that UEFA will be more wary of any attempt to circumvent the rules.
People familiar with the matter said on Thursday there had been no prior discussions with Paris-Saint-Germain on Neymar’s transfer.
All of them were only in contact with The Associated Press in an unnamed capacity because they were not authorized to discuss the state of Saint-Germain Football Club in Paris.
According to the last published accounts, the Paris Saint-Germain Football Club's operating income in 2015-16 was 542.4 million euros, with a profit of more than 10 million euros.
The accounts for the previous fiscal year had not yet been released.
Paris Saint-Germain Football Club expects Neymar’s presence to be a boon for sponsorship events, franchises and jersey sales.
The club is expected to increase revenue by at least 30 per cent after signing Neymar.
It’s clear to see one of the most well-known players leave, the Spanish league is deeply outraged to try to block Neymar’s move and claim that Paris Saint-Germain Football Club is in violation of the rules.
La Liga president Havel Tebas told the Spanish newspaper AS that “this is a clear example of a ‘financial stimulant’ that the club and the country are the same”, referring to Qatar, which has ultimate ownership of Paris-Saint-Germain Football Club.
Arsene Wenger, head coach of the Arsenal side (who owned the club for the United States), had reservations about state-sponsored clubs and La Liga.
“It’s a consequence of ownership, and it’s revolutionizing the entire ecology of football for the last 15 years,” Wenger said on Thursday.
“As soon as the country owns the club, then anything can happen.
This will make it very difficult to comply with the Rules, because for a country there are many different ways or kinds of interests to require big players to represent a country.”
Intelligent interactive robot developer Rokid received a new investment
In recent years, start-ups in the field of artificial intelligence have continued to be popular with capital.
Artificial intelligence Rokid recently completed a new round of financing, led by Temasek, Credit Suisse, CDIB (the investment institution of Taiwan's China Development Finance Holding Company), old shareholder IDG Capital and other institutions.
It is reported that the scale of this round of financing is about 100 million U.S. dollars, Credit Suisse and CDIB for the first time in the field of domestic artificial intelligence financing transactions.
Rokid is an intelligent interactive robot developer, committed to the field of robot research, the product can control the home intelligent equipment, with continuous independent learning and growth functions, but also with recognition, understanding, feedback and other functions.
This round of financing will be mainly used for research and development, talent introduction, strategic promotion and investment layout of the industrial chain.
Previously, in August 2014, Rokid received $8.3 million angel investment from IDG, linear capital, Mfund and Yuanjing Capital. In October 2015, the company also received tens of millions of US dollars A round of investment led by Huadeng International and angel investors; on November 1, 2016, Rokid completed a B round of investment led by Shangke Capital, IDG, Mfund, Yuanjing Capital, Huaden International and other institutions.
At the time, Rokid said that the company's valuation had reached 450 million U.S. dollars after the B round of financing.
In May 2017, Rokid released the consumer-grade intelligent voice robot "Juki Moonstone", which combines artificial intelligence and home scenes for a price of 1399 yuan.
This robot uses speech synthesis, natural language understanding, speech recognition technology and microphone array technology, with chat conversations, playing music, smart home functions.
He has won two awards at the International Consumer Electronics Show (CES) Tech For A Better World and Home Audio/Video Components And Accessories, as well as the German IF Design Award, which is known as an Oscar for design.
At the just-concluded 2018 CES, Rokid released a new product: consumer-grade augmented reality AR glasses RokidGlass.
Used as a stand-alone headset without the need to connect to a smartphone or an external computer, it is expected to be commercialized in 2018.
Previously, the company has released RokidAlien, the first-generation home robot, and RokidPebble, the second-generation smart speaker.
