According to Apple, starting from February 28 this year, the iCloud service in mainland China will be operated by Guizhou on the cloud.
In a statement, Apple said, “Collaboration with Guizhou in the cloud will allow us to continue to improve the speed and reliability of iCloud services, as well as compliance with the new regulations that Chinese cloud services must be operated by local enterprises.”
During the deactivation period, the user's data previously stored in iCloud will also be stored in iCloud and will not be lost.
If the user wishes to use the iCloud service after February 28, they will need to accept the new terms and conditions first to ensure that the account can be reactivated and moved to Guizhou.
At the same time, Apple also stressed in the statement that "Apple has a strong data privacy and security protection mechanism, and will not create backdoors in any of our systems."
The main data center of iCloud Gui'an New District will also be Apple's first data center project set up in China, and after the completion of the project, it will be used to store the data of Apple users in China.
When it is completed and put into use, Chinese users do not need to upload and download the data back and forth across the long distances between China and the United States, increasing the speed and reducing the delay.
