Nostalgia Infectious Infectious Market - "Hara Summer Camp" and "Detective Twin雄s"
The retelling of the story is nostalgic for actors in the 1980s as well as the garbage days of the ’90’s (David Wayne and Michael Schuwart’s sketch comedy The Nation for MTV); it’s also a nostalgia of nostalgia itself, a nostalgia for the year of 2001; a year in which stars such as Elisabeth Banks, Amy Poehler, and Paul Luther have yet.
As a kind of cultural art, it is the imitation and embodiment of the permanent memories of our Facebook era.
But as a series, it felt like there wasn’t enough time.
The first day of the camp was well-received, but it wasn’t just because of its grotesque poignant hustles (the plot is an original story about canned vegetables that speaks, voiced by H. Jon Benjamin).
Doubling the use of the core laughing terroir - a middle-of-the-life actor to play a trick-or-treating child - this makes the inability to go back into the past also become a funny part.
Ten years later, when adults actually play as adults, the emotions are not too deep-seated, and the imitation is even more rambling, including the impromptu composition of the 90's tracking movies, such as "Pulling the Cradle".
At some point, a character gets caught up in an Apple notebook, some straight-line skates, and “something else from 1991.”
Many of the core stories are the same.
He is now a novelist, but he still hasn’t forgotten Katie (Margaret Morrow).
Victor (Ken Marino) still has a virgin.
There’s also a sinister plot involving Ronald Reagan (also played by Mr. Hewitt), instigated by George H.W. Bush (Michael Ian Black).
As always, the best part of this series is interjecting.
It’s especially interesting to see this ever-young Mr. Luther, who flaunted Matt Dillon’s soul patch in the movie Single Noble, and Andy in the previous “Campus Giants” now feels like a giant.
It’s a moment of stochastic madness that could power the film, like a recursive seven-year-old series, until I come to what I thought was a logical conclusion: “Hara Summer Camp: Twenty Years Later.”
At the same time, Amazon's "Detective duo" tells a variety of nostalgic content: Cold War rejuvenated "American spy dream", the movie "Cold city", and even "GLOW: gorgeous women's wrestling alliance" in the east-west wrestling image.
This comedy, created by Brian Gatwood and Alessandro Tanaka, was put on the television screen by director Rees Thomas: a sibling comedy set in Romania in the 1980s and tells the story of two Bucharest officials who struggle for the lines of truth, atheism, and Marxism-Leninism.
The main characters - the hardcore Grigor and the idealistic Eisoff - were played by Florin Pirsci and Cornelius Ullić, respectively, whose dialogues were voiced in English by Chaning Tatum (also an executive producer) and Joseph Gordon-Levitt in English.
It's a wonderful idea.
But it’s not just an idea.
There are some good visual gimmicks (the cop spends time playing chess while watching TV because Americans like to play football).
And there are a number of celebrity voice actors – including Jeanne Sreette, Nick Overman, MahSarah Hashbaz Ali and Kolo Sepwini – to create a spectacular sound extravaganza.
However, “Detective duo” is so committed to creating a raucous hustle-and-roiding promotional comedy – with a killer wearing a Reagan mask – and it usually plays such a role.
The six-episode-long TV was a bit procrastinated, and the comedic effects didn’t release quickly or frequently enough.
"Detective duo" is more strictly edited, when Gregor and Eisoff repeatedly visited the U.S. Embassy, showing a better satirical effect, because there are always two fat people gorging a bunch of hamburgers in the embassy lobby.
For imaginary communist spectators, this message means that Western culture can turn anything into fiery material.
There is nothing wrong with the creators of this fictitious Romanian theme, and we will even use our modern history and their history to tell stories.
The reporter recently interviewed and learned that the efficiency of steel, rail transit, shipbuilding, machinery manufacturing and other industries in the northeast has generally declined, and there have even been losses.
Some experts believe that under the current situation, a new round of rejuvenation in the northeast industry is not only related to the country's current round of economic recovery, it will also have an impact on the long-term development of China's manufacturing industry and international competitiveness, so it is urgent to adjust and transform.
The efficiency of the traditional industry is now a serious decline or even a loss in the efficiency of the raw materials industry and the heavy machinery and equipment manufacturing industry, which is the primary performance of the northeast industry in the current round of economic downturn.
In the past, in the economic downturn, it was mainly the characteristics of low-end and primary products, but in this economic fluctuation, the high-end and low-end industrial products in the northeast were affected.
In the past few years from the adjustment of the product structure began, focusing on the development of high-end plates such as container boards, steel rails, shipboards and civil household appliances, but this year, the sales of these varieties have declined significantly, especially the sales of shipboards fell by 50% compared with the same period last year.
Shao Qingshun, head of the metallurgical department of the Liaoning Provincial Industry and Information Technology Commission, said that metallurgy is the third pillar industry in Liaoning, with an annual steel production capacity of 60 million tons ranked third in the country.
From January to May this year, Liaoning coarse-steel production of 21.54 million tons, the production and sales of products required by national policies and strong local government construction projects are in a good situation, and the decline of products with fierce market competition is relatively large, which is another feature of the northeastern manufacturing industry.
