The Bank of England will maintain an interest rate of 0.25%.
The Bank of England is due to announce at a later date that interest rates will remain unchanged, after weak economic growth and accommodative inflation dashed hopes.
The Bank of England has warned that economic growth will remain “weak” as it tightens household incomes while reining in interest rates.
Policy-makers on the bank’s Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) voted 6-2 to maintain the 0.25 per cent rate, while a small number of members this month have called for higher interest rates, even as weak economic growth has rattled.
In its quarterly inflation report, the Bank of England shrunk its growth forecasts for 2017 to 1.7 per cent and 1.6 per cent, respectively, and issued a warning that a contraction in household income would continue, while inflation would skyrocket to nearly 3 per cent in the autumn.
In the next few years, however, it said interest rates would need to be raised to contain inflation caused by Brexit, while borrowing costs would likely need to be raised more sharply in financial markets.
A few years ago, a large-scale economic promotion policy was implemented due to the impact of Brexit, and members also voted to withdraw it.
The Bank of England will announce the suspension of its fixed-term financing plan, which will provide cheap funds to banks from February next year, although the bank said that under the plan, the bank now needs an additional £15 billion on top of the £115 billion.
In the minutes of interest-rate decision-making meetings, the Bank of England said: “In the MPC’s core projections, gross domestic product (GDP) remains weak in the near term, as tighter real household incomes will continue to dampen consumption.”
With regard to interest rates, the bank has repeatedly reiterated the need for “certain monetary policy tightening” to suppress inflation, and to a “greater degree” than the market expects.
At present, the market is expected to achieve the first growth in the third quarter of next year and the second in 2020.
However, the bank stressed that any increase will be "slow" and "restraint".
The bank’s growth forecasts for this year and next showed a downward trend, compared to 1.9% and 1.7% in May.
The bank maintained its growth forecast of 1.8% in 2018.
The exchange rate of the British pound fell against the US dollar, followed by the euro.
The British pound fell 0.5% against the U.S. dollar to 1.31, while against the euro fell 0.4% to 1.11.
Prior to the unchanged decision, rising expectations were dashed by the recent lackluster growth figures. GDP rose to 0.3% in the second quarter, compared with just 0.2% in the previous three months.
The bank said growth in the third quarter is likely to remain at 0.3%, although the end of the year will increase slightly.
Ordinary households have been buffeted by skyrocketing inflation, while wage growth has been negligible, and the Bank of England’s most recent inflation reports haven’t had good news for them, as the bank says austerity will get worse before it’s getting better.
He said monetary policy “doesn’t prevent” a crackdown on income over the next few years, but that wages are expected to recover “massively” at the end of a three-year forecast.
At the same time, because of the depreciation of the pound, the demand for British goods has increased significantly, and the economy will be stimulated to offset some of the impact of low consumption.
The decision came a year after an astounding EU referendum sent the pound plunging, with interest rates cut to 0.25 per cent in August.
The Bank of England estimates it could take up to four years to fully have an impact on prices and the UK economy.
Before the bank’s latest interest-rate ruling was released, calls for interest-rate hikes continued, as inflation added to the already stressed average household, while three policymakers called for an interest rate to rise to 0.5 percent in July.
Inflation retreated to 2.6% last month from 2.9% in June, although the Bank of England said it was expected and would rise again in the next few months.
Compared with its 2% target, the bank said that the excess is already the final bottom line of a weak sterling.
Coreco Mortgage Broker’s director, Andrew Monterick, said: “The mortgage market looks set to become even more competitive again in light of the recent drop in swap rates, as lenders strongly call for more business and look forward to putting the perfect end this year with a positive momentum.”
Such a market looks particularly favorable for those who may be nearing the end of an existing deal, as remortgages (many of which are fee-free and have low interest rates) are becoming increasingly popular.
