The UK’s National Health Service (NHS)’s controversial HIV drugs are giving the green light.
With the dust settled in the High Court’s provision of drug funding for the NHS, patients will be able to receive “transformative” HIV prophylaxis starting in September.
NHS England has announced that pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP), a drug that can be taken prior to sex, has been shown to reduce the risk of infection in high-risk groups by around 86%. The health service will now deliver the drug to an estimated 10,000 people through an initial three-year trial.
It agreed to fund the trial after an appeals court upheld a high court ruling last year. The ruling said the NHS was empowered to pay the PrEP costs, despite the NHS's claim that local authorities should fund the drug.
Thursday’s announcement was described as a “significant new intervention” by NHS chief executive Simon Stevens, while the National AIDS Trust (NAT) described it as “a critical moment”.
Sexual health clinics in places such as London, Brighton, Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield will be among the first to be registered. More clinics will be added in October before being fully implemented in England in April 2018.
Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) is an HIV drug that is taken prior to sex to reduce the risk of HIV infection.
Experiments have shown that PrEP can significantly reduce HIV transmission in people at risk of infection.
Taking PrEP prior to sex means that there are enough specific medications in the human body to prevent an HIV infection.
The drug can be taken every day or two hours prior to sexual intercourse.
Those who are eligible for medical treatment include lesbian, gay and bisexual men, transgender people, as well as masculine HIV carriers who have unsuccessfully received treatment.
The clinic will identify eligible and consent-to-experiment participants, including men, women, transgender people, as well as those whose sexual partners have an unknown status for HIV infection and who are receiving antiretroviral treatment.
People who live and register as a general practitioner in England can also sign up at a sexual health clinic where they attend.
The scheme will cost £10 million, while the NHS England branch has signed a contract to procure the drugs this week after an internationally competitive sourcing.
The trial was designed to assess the full potential of PrEP by collecting clinical evidence on a large-sample basis on how it is localized, absorbed, and implemented.
“This significant new intervention should be a broad-based and increasingly successful complementary and reinforcing role in the prevention of HIV.”
This is yet another milestone for more than 30 years of action worthy of progress in addressing the major human health challenges.”
Deborah King, chief executive of the British AIDS Trust (NAT), said: "This is a critical moment in the fight against AIDS.
Pre-exposure prophylaxis offers the possibility of changing the HIV epidemic in the UK if it is indeed targeted at people in need and at risk.
Starting in September, people at high risk of living with HIV will be available through an NHS-funded trial conducted in England with a new enabling tool that can be truly implemented individually and without consultation with sexual partners, which will improve the lives of so many people.
We are in favour of this announcement with both hands.”
Ian Green, chief executive of the Terence Higgins Trust, said: "The priority now is to make sure that the trials are rapidly rolled out across the country and that none of the people living with HIV can fall.
“Now that we’ve bought PrEP, we’re ready to protect more than 10,000 people at high risk of HIV.”
Shadow Public Health Minister Sharon Hodgson said: "After several months of delays and the government's procrastination, PrEP's start-up is very popular and should have come earlier."
There is evidence of how this drug can change as part of our approach to preventing HIV and stopping the spread of this deadly infectious disease.
“This trial will allow us to go a step further and fully understand the benefits of PrEP.”
It’s now important that this trial be rolled out nationally as soon as possible in order to protect those who come into contact with HIV and to help us go one step further to end the spread of HIV in society.”
