High quality of life: application of lofts and clever design to create a small apartment in the Count's palace into a spacious home
Joanne Ray was a former banker who was very interested in renovating the house, and in 2014, she moved from her large house on Knightsbridge to a small apartment in the Count’s Palace.
This apartment is located on the first floor of an elegant Queen Anne-style terrace – Joanne didn’t pack her bags and sold it, which is a powerful testament to the new design.
“Before that, my ex-boyfriend and I were living in a much bigger house on Knightsbridge,” Joanne said.
“I was looking for the right house.
I saw this apartment in the Count’s Palace, and its ceiling was also very high.
"I'll make an offer the same day."
The 1,000-square-foot apartment has now been completely overhauled since its first visit.
The apartment used to be a cosmopolitan room that needed to go through the kitchen to get to the bedroom, and Joanne wanted to add storage space and loft to take full advantage of the ample ceiling height.
“I wanted high ceilings, big windows, lots of light.
That’s what I’m asking for,” she said.
To this end, she hired Erfan Azadi from Duck & Shed's architecture and design studio (Notting Hill) to take advantage of this 12.5-foot-tall ceiling to create a home. This home is not only a development project, but also a place where Joanne lives.
The height of the ceiling is not large enough to add two upper and lower spaces, so the architectural challenge is to create an attic high enough to stand.
Erfan had to think creatively in order to solve the problem.
“The steel structure is quite complex,” he explained.
“We have to arrange the space so that it’s displayed up and down, and we don’t have to shrug our shoulders.
It’s not as simple as building a simple platform, it’s about starting these steels so that they’re at the right height to achieve functionality.”
Through this arrangement, Erfan was able to gain access to two full-body-height rooms in the space – which can add a lot of interesting elements.
The loft features Retrouvius brand’s retro copper windows overlooking the enchanting living area.
Golden furniture with mirrors, silver piece furniture and Serge Mouille's retro lighting form the overall style.
The use of mirrors plays an important role in increasing atmosphere, space and light.
Put a mirror on both ends of the short hallway, which can make it look longer.
In the kitchen, a splash board with a mirror is like a window leading to another room.
At the porch, small lattices with mirrors provide Joanne with storage space for utensils and glassware.
The beautiful living room has an attractive sense of space, which is able to put the kitchen away when the kitchen is unused.
The folding kitchen is not new.
But what’s unique about this folding kitchen is that it’s a garage-style lift door.
“There’s a lot of knowledge in the field of engineering,” Erfan explained the steel reinforcement veneer MDF door.
“It’s based on the principle of balance, so it’s easy to swipe when you push the door.”
Due to the clever use of space and inventory, Joanne received an additional 16 square meters, an additional bedroom, and an improved layout.
“Ultimately, what you’re selling is the size of the house — or the valuation of your home is based on the size of the house,” Erfan said.
“Basically, a real estate agent walks into a room and calculates it based on floor size.”
They will try to increase the space occupied while using high-quality materials, thus bringing a sense of luxury to the space.
Erfan said, “Materials are hand-picked by you, so it helps to create an emotional bond at home.”
“You won’t fall in love with the power on the wall.
All of these things are important, but they won't entrentize you."
It turns out that with a good design as well as the addition of extra space, a standard apartment can be transformed to amaze you.
In fact, like Joanne, you may choose to live.
