The two brothers staged a $500 million real estate empire scramble
Forty years after the founding of their Italian families, the two brothers fought a battle for one of Victoria’s largest real estate empires worth more than $500 million.
Colin De Lutis, the former director and developer of Carleton Football Club, lived in a mansion in Tukala, and the distance between the three doors was the residence of his younger brother Paul. The latter took him to the Supreme Court regarding the separation of their real estate and operating assets.
Since 2014, Paul has been hoping to break away from the family business, but the two brothers are engaged in a heated debate in the Supreme Court about the asset split.
Together, the brothers and their mother have 38 properties worth more than $500 million, accumulated since the family’s purchase of Smith Street in Collingwood District in 1973.
That’s when the family founded Westco Jeans and grew it to the size of its 160 retail chains.
In 1999, it was sold for $85 million.
Since then, that money has been put into the real estate. Currently, the DeRutis family’s asset portfolio includes city office buildings, Brunswick and Sorrento development projects, Gold Coast and Bühler Hill apartments, and suburban shopping malls.
According to an agreement reached with his late father, Colin, known to his younger brother Paul as the “principal mover and dominant figure” of the DeRutis family, owns about two-thirds of the company’s assets, while Paul accounts for about a third.
According to documents submitted to the court, it was calculated that the property owned by Colin was worth $316 million, while the property owned by Paul was worth $176 million.
According to Paul, the separation of real estate and cash proved to be the crux of the negotiations.
“Colin’s proposal for the division of property leaves what I think is the best,” he wrote in an initial court affidavit in 2015.
On Friday, the Supreme Court held its hearing for the first time after several mediation failures.
The two brothers are still vying for control of the existing business, and Paul said Colin had kicked him out.
Colin denied this and accused Paul of poor management of certain real estate properties.
In an early affidavit submitted by Colin, he expressed a desire to resolve the dispute.
“My Italian tradition dictates that family is the most important option. My Italian ancestry determines that family is the most important,” he wrote.
There are hundreds of pages of affidavits, e-mails, and corporate reports submitted by companies such as Deloitte, Pitcher Partners, Charter Keck Cramer, Allan Myers, QC, as well as senior lawyers.
Madame de Rutis and her late husband, Luigi, were both Italian immigrants. From the end of the 1950s, they started running mass restaurants and deli, and bought a shop in Braunschweig in the early 1970s.
They used collateral to buy the Collingwood property, where Colin opened the family’s first jeans store.
Colin said, “When I was in high school,” where he founded Westco, “when Paul was very young (about 10 years old).
Anna de Routis said she and Luigi, who died in 2012, wanted to be able to pass the property to her son in peace.
“Luigi used to say to me that he never wanted his brothers to go to court in the end,” she wrote.
The other two siblings, Mark and Luke, are no longer members of the DeRutis group, and Colin said that when Mark left the company, they paid him $10 million and Luke for $26 million when he left the company.
Paul said that both Luke and Mark were “dissatisfied with the terms of their property division.”
According to television star Eddie McGuire in a biography published in 2015, Colin has kept a low profile despite being a former member of the AFL club’s board.
“Fairfax Media” contacted Colin and Paul on Friday, as well as their lawyers.
However, it was only Colin who responded by text. He said that because the case had been brought to court, "I can't really say too much."
“It’s very, very distressing that my younger brother Paul chose to sue me and bring our family affairs to court,” he wrote.
In mid-2016, Colin wrote in a court filing that he had tried to meet his brother’s demands.
“Our father didn’t want to see Paul and me end up in such a dispute,” he said, adding that he “remains hopeful about the resolution of the dispute.”
On Friday, that hope seemed dashed.
“Our problems will be resolved through the courts in the near future,” Colin wrote.
