Trump’s new chief of staff plans to limit the president’s media time
The others have tried, but have all failed.
John F. Kelley might actually be able to do that.
If that’s the case, he’ll be the first.
Politico, a U.S.-based political news website, said the new White House chief of staff planned to restrict access to President Trump’s messages — including news media reports — in an effort to keep big bosses more stable.
Here’s a quote from journalist Josh Dawcy:
“When the new White House chief of staff, John F. Kelley, started his first day of discussions with senior officials, he pointed out that he wanted to solve one of the main problems that President Trump had in the White House: the bad information that flowed into the hands of the president.”
Kelley told these officials that receiving excellent information would help the president make better decisions, so the information needed to go through his hands — both in writing and in the form of a briefing.
Kelley’s diagnosis may look like this, but others have tried to tame it by spying on Trump’s media time, but it all ended in failure.
President Donald Trump’s TV addiction has a long history—now in the White House, and his habit of watching TV is still deepening.
In February, former campaign adviser Sam Naberger told Politico: "If candidate Trump is frustrated by unfair reporting, it will be very helpful to let him know that he also has positive coverage in credible channels."
Politico’s Tara Palmieri wrote at the time that “based on the experience of the six former campaign officials, controlling Trump’s habit of tweeting focuses on ensuring that the media messages he personally receives contain a certain amount of praise.”
That's no problem at all.
But it’s ludicrous that Trump’s Twitter habits used to be “controlled.”
It’s possible that these campaign officials know something we don’t know—that’s if it weren’t for their interference, Trump’s tweets would be even hotter.
“We probably never knew the tweets Trump didn’t send.
If his staff were able to save him from the fire, even if only a few times, their efforts would be worth it.
However, no one has been able to prevent Trump from causing controversy.
Part of the problem is that in the White House, which is made up of competing blocs, people promote their world view by handing Trump with information that may or may not be reliable.
Politico — all about the story — reported in May on advisers’ hobbies for strategically providing questionable information to the president.
Here’s an example described by journalist Sean Goodman:
According to current and former Trump officials, Trump’s reaction to negative news clips such as volcanic eruptions, especially those with destructive information leaks, will be frantically trying to figure out its source.
At the end of February, someone maliciously photocopied an article on GotNews.com, the website of network agitator Charles C. Johnson, to the president, which accused deputy chief of staff Katie Walsh of the White House of "the source of a large amount of information leaks" at that time.
Even if Johnson has been permanently banned from Twitter for harassment without providing any concrete evidence, he has blown up a large number of false allegations and later openly denied it.
Mr. Trump looked at the article and started asking officials about Walsh.”
Goodmancher also noted that Reines Prybass, the chief of staff at the time, and White House secretary Rob Porter “attempted to set up a system to manage and record the documents Trump received.”
What was the result?
Kelley was going to do the same, after three months.
He may be able to prove that he’s a more efficient manager than Prybas, but Trump is the impulsive Trump, and his aides are still competing with each other for influence.
These timeless influencing factors will make Kelly’s task even more difficult.
