Motorhomes Negotiate on ditches to return to ‘Rich Street’
Some of the West Midlands region’s richest owners are at loggerheads clashing with determined motorhomes over a government-excavated gully for the return of land beyond the mansion.
Motorhomes have parked their 20 trailers in an open space outside the bustling Solihuel Street, which is dubbed “Plution Street.”
Motorhomes, armed with their horses in their arms, bogging their babies and leading motorhome convoys, returned again after being evicted in May, and the “moat” dug up by the municipal authorities didn’t prevent them from entering.
This sleek and sophisticated team found that there was a gap in this defensive line, and although there was no roadblock to pass, their vehicles were squeezed through the gap.
Furious residents have fiercely criticized the Solihull Council, saying it was “very poor in dealing with the issue.”
A resident, who did not want to be named, said: "They were there on Monday night before coming to Bentley Heath Street and throwing stones at passers-by.
The committee should be ashamed of what they are doing, because they are simply useless in dealing with issues.
The RVers came here in May, were driven out, and now they’re back again.
There's been a lot last night, and it's going to be more today."
She added: “The Birmingham City Council knows how to deal with it by imposing a long-term ban to protect its parks.
“I live on Streetsbrook Road, and if Parliament can’t prevent something like this, I’m not willing to keep paying municipal taxes.
A local resident, speaking on condition of anonymity, said: “It’s so ridiculous, we just saw the last group of motorhomes gone, just to make it easier for another batch to be destroyed.”
This time, the number has increased, and some people have even seen horses grazing on the park's lawn.”
“We are aware of an unlicensed encampment in an open area of Szrütsbruck Road and we are now working with Solihull police and taking the necessary legal action to allow them to move away as soon as possible.”
In order to support our collection of evidence, it’s very important to ask as many local residents as possible to report any anti-social or criminal behavior to the police by calling 101.
This will help us to provide an overview of the situation when we submit our case to the Court for its examination.”
On March 15, the 5th meeting of the 12th National People's Congress held its closing session at the Great Hall of the People in Beijing.
This is an interview with the Director of the National Post Office, Ma Junsheng.
Xinhua News Agency: At 9 a.m. on March 15, the 5th meeting of the 12th National People's Congress held a closing meeting in the Great Hall of the People.
Before the closing ceremony, Ma Junsheng, director of the State Post Bureau, said in an interview in the "ministerial channel" that this year, in view of the outstanding problems such as express delays in express delivery in the express delivery industry and the loss and destruction of couriers, the state will strengthen supervision and guide the strengthening of industry self-discipline.
This year will strengthen efforts to solve the outstanding problems of the four major express delivery industries Ma Junsheng said that the development of China's express delivery industry has now entered the fast lane.
In 2012, China's express delivery volume was 5.6 billion pieces, and 10,000 express three-wheeled vehicles were on the road, implementing unified standards, unified logos, and unified insurance, which not only facilitates delivery, but also ensures safety.
