Italy adopts tough stance on NGO migrant rescue ship
Italy has issued a warning to non-governmental organizations operating migrant rescue ships in the Mediterranean that they will not be able to continue to implement the rescue if the new operational management norms are not signed.
In an interview with the Turin-based daily News, the Minister of the Interior, Marco Minniti, stated: “If NGOs do not sign new codes of conduct, it is difficult to see how they will continue.”
Minnetti’s warning came a day after Italian officials detained him on suspicions that the crew of a German aid group, Youth Rescue, was actually colluding with human traffickers to help illegal immigrants.
The aid group operated for only one year and declined to comment on the specific subject of the allegations by Italian prosecutor Ambrogio Cartosio.
In a statement on social media, it said: “Saving lives is and will continue to be the most important thing for us, so we regret not being able to operate in search-and-rescue zones at this time.”
“After all the information has been collected, we can only assess all the allegations and the situation at this time.”
Since October, Italian officials have been spying on the Iuventa, a ship organized by Youth Relief.
Its crews are suspected of carrying migrants carried by human traffickers directly from small boats and allowing smugglers to flee hastily and re-utilize the ships.
At least one such meeting allegedly took place 1.3 miles off the coast of Libya, according to the prosecutor's records. The contents of the document were published by Italian media.
Its crew is accused of blatantly ignoring the authority of the Italian Coast Guard, which oversees rescue operations in the area out of humanitarian zeal rather than for any other motive.
In accordance with the code of conduct, vessels such as Iuventa clearly require an Italian police officer to supervise their activities on board.
At present, only three of the nine non-governmental organizations involved in search-and-rescue operations have agreed to abide by the code of conduct: Save the Children, the Malta-based Migrant Maritime Assistance Station and Spain’s Pro-Activa Open Arms.
The latter said on Thursday it believed the new rules, while not necessary, were acceptable because they did not pose any “significant changes or hindrances” to its relief operations.
Among the organizations that refused to sign was the Nobel Prize-winning organization Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF).
At the same time, Minniti said that the number of migrants arriving in Italy has plummeted in recent weeks, which shows that efforts to strengthen Libyan coastal guards and unite local mayors are beginning to bear fruit.
“In the last few days, we have begun to see the dawn at the end of the tunnel.”
He unveiled plans for this month’s in-depth talks with Libyan mayors to discuss economic development and with Chad, Niger and Mali on ways to reduce the number of migrants who want to leave those countries for Europe.
