Michelle Carter Sentence Sentenced to Comand of Comrad Roy's Suspicion
A three-and-a-half-year sentence was sentenced to two-and-a-half years for abusing her boyfriend to commit suicide.
The 20-year-to-year-old Michelle Carter will serve a sentence of 15 months, after which she will be converted to a reprievous sentence.
In June, Carter was convicted of inadvertent and unpremeditated crimes against Conrad Roy, who committed suicide on July 13, 2014.
Speaking at a hearing on Thursday, Roy’s father said: “My family is heartbroken.
“My son is my best friend.”
While awaiting his appeal, Carter was granted personal freedom.
She faces up to 20 years in prison, but her team of lawyers believes that she and Roy are both mentally ill.
At the sentencing, Carter stood, wearing red trousers and a beige jersey, clasping his hands with tears in his eyes.
“Her actions killed Conrad Roy,” prosecutors told the court.
“She ended his life for the better.
“She was not responsible,” the prosecutor said.
“She doesn’t have any remorse.”
But Carter's lawyer said it was "a terrible consequence and she deeply regrets it."
Judge Lawrence Moniz, however, stated that he did not find that her “age or level of maturity, or even her mental illness, would have had any significant impact on her behavior”.
A prosecutor read a statement from Lynn Roy, Roy’s mother, saying, “I didn’t have a single day without being saddened by the loss of my beloved son.
“I want him to be proud of me and everything I’ve done. I’m trying to stand with his sisters in all my pain that will accompanies us forever,” the statement said.
Camdyn Roy, Roy’s sister, testified that she was “mentally thorny” because she realized that she could never attend her brother’s wedding or become his child’s aunt.
“The loss of someone who has been with me since I was born is a kind of pain, and the rest of my life will be tied to that pain,” she said.
The case appears to have set a legal precedent, because the Massachusetts law does not provide for abetting anyone else to commit suicide.
In 2014, Roy was found to have died of carbon monoxide poisoning in his car; that year, Carter was 17 years old.
After Carter’s text messages revealed she instiating him to commit suicide, the case drew national attention.
“There are plenty of ways to hang, jump, stab yourself,” she said in a number of text messages sent two weeks before his death, when he was on vacation with his family.
In the period leading up to his suicide, she wrote: "You need to do that, Conrad, "All you have to do is start the generator, and then you'll be free and happy."
Judge Moniz said Carter gained attention through Mr. Roy’s death and ordered her not to profit from the case through any media.
There are differences of legal opinion in this case.
Amanda Knox is one of the few who defended Carter, who was acquitted two years after she was accused of murdering her British roommate in Italy in 2007.
Ms. Knox, 30, said in an op-ed published in the Los Angeles Times: “Michelle Carter deserves sympathy and help, not imprisonment.”
