Automobile manufacturers face impact, Audi will convert up to 850,000 diesel vehicles
Audi’s announcement came a day after BMW said it would offer software upgrades for more than 350,000 vehicles.
BMW’s chairman said it believed there were “more intelligent options for driving rather than restrictions”, meaning that major cities in Europe – including Munich, where the company’s headquarters are located – are considering banning or restricting diesel vehicles.
Daimler announced on Tuesday that it would retrofit Europe’s 3 million Mercedes cars to reduce its diesel emissions.
Not a single company said the move was a recall.
The use of diesel vehicles by European car manufacturers in Europe and the United States to help meet CO2 emission regulations.
However, nitrogen oxides emitted by diesel engines are thought to be carcinogenic and can lead to asthma.
Automobile companies are also installing equipment to offset the cost of exhaust emissions of diesel vehicles is also increasing, so it is difficult to maintain the competitiveness of diesel vehicles in terms of price.
As German carmakers face censoring, Chancellor Angela Merkel’s government has also been accused of allowing these powerful car companies and ignoring signs of the problem.
These car companies are trying to avoid a repeat of Volkswagen’s mistake of using so-called failed devices, adjusting their emissions during vehicle testing to comply with regulations and returning to normal driving conditions.
A number of Volkswagen executives have been charged in the U.S., while others are under investigation from countries on both sides of the Atlantic.
Last month, the former head of thermodynamics at Audi’s engine development department was arrested in Germany.
Zachero Giovanni Pamio, who is an Italian and is therefore not subject to extradition and could face trial in the United States.
Earlier this year, Audi was embroiled in a German criminal investigation involving Volkswagen, in which the German government accused it of installing a system to evade emissions requirements in Europe, expanding the scope of the investigation, which was previously limited to the United States.
Audi’s investigation is likely to have a significant impact on VW’s: luxury carmakers account for a disproportionate share of Volkswagen’s profits.
From the preparation to the release of a film, which lasted for one or two or even a few years, it can be described as commonplace.
The animated film "Big Fish Haitang" has recently announced that it will be released on July 8, and this film will take 12 years from preparation to release!
The original author of "Big Fish Haitang" Liang, 21-year-old from Tsinghua University's thermal power industry, together with his friend Zhang Chun to create the other side of the sky, in 2008 won the joint venture policy source of millions of US dollars of venture capital, launched the animated film debut "Big Fish Haitang".
The film has won many awards at home and abroad in the creative sample stage, and has been included and praised as “one of the most outstanding works of art” by the Centre Pompidou Contemporary Art in France.
Due to the six-year period from creativity to production, the film has been referred to as the "god pit" by millions of fans who are looking forward to the release.
"Big Fish Haitang" tells a fantasy story that belongs to the Chinese people. With the release of the trailer, it also brings new expectations to all audiences.
Liang said: "These 12 years have only done this one thing, and now it has changed from a dream to a reality, and I am both nervous and excited."
Zhang Chun said: "always want to use 50% of other people's budget to make exactly the same picture effect as others, and the team will be driven crazy by me."
