Provence lavender threatened by climate change
Lavender has become a symbol of the city of Provence in southern France, but there’s bad news that it’s threatened by climate change.
Lavender is fading due to persistent droughts and the emergence of virus-carrying insects, a headache for scientists and farmers who are trying to find a solution.
Eric Chaisse, an expert at the Agricultural Research Institute in Provence (CRIEPPAM), said: “Lavender in Provence is facing a huge threat, as in recent years the plant has been affected by the effects of the effects of climate change, with heavy rainfall in the spring and heavy rain in the autumn.
This can affect lavender’s life cycle.
There’s also a smaller individual cicadas, leaf cicadas that can spread tiny germs that appear in large numbers in very dry weather.”
The important thing is that their numbers are huge.
In addition to attracting vacationers, lavender oil is also sought after by the perfume and cosmetics industry.
Eryker Zeiss said: "Contrary to plants that can open a large number of large flowers and have a very long grain, we have the problem of smaller individuals, poor development, missing or weak."
That's why we're having fewer essential oils."
The solutions under study include adopting better irrigation techniques and slowing the evaporation of soil moisture, sprinkling protective mixtures of clay and water on plants, and developing more drought-tolerant varieties.
