In a dangerous country, journalists have been killed.
All employees of Riodoce Weekly typically rallied on Wednesday to review their plans to cover the Sinalo riots, caused by criminal organizations, corrupt officials, and an endless war on drugs.
But today, the shadow of tragedy hangs over them, and they come together to discuss security issues.
It’s important that they’ve been told to change their daily habits.
Two veteran journalists discussed what makes them feel safer: whether to bring their children to the office, which was attacked by a hand grenade in 2009, or whether they should be kept at home.
Security experts wrote three words on the blackboard in front of the room: Enemies, Neutrators, and Allies.
They asked the journalists to name them under each column—no evidence was needed, observation and intuition were sufficient.
Suspected drug dealers, politicians, businessmen and journalists on the government’s list, or by cartels, the scoundrels threshing that Mexico’s reporting jeopardises.
There have never been more and more people in the country, including more and more journalists: at least 25 journalists have been killed since President Enrique Peña Nieto took office in December 2012, 589 journalists have received federal protection following attacks and threats, and at least seven journalists have died in seven states so far this year.
The last person to die was their editors and inspiration, and Javier Valdez Cárdenas, the co-founder of Riodoce.
Vardez, who has written many books on drug-related violence, wrote in one: “The biggest mistake is to live in Mexico and become a journalist.”
You can deeply feel that he’s gone, even though he’s everywhere—a huge photo hanging on the obverse of Riodoce’s office building, Valdez with his middle finger, with the words “righteousness”; the T-shirts worn by Aaron and Miriam Ramirez, two T-shirts in their thirties, showed his grin, wearing glasses or his iconic Panama straw hat.
Less than two months after his death, journalists discussed their shared traumas, nightmares, insomnia and skepticism.
Although a special federal prosecutor's office was set up in 2010 to deal with the cases of journalists, according to the records of the Committee for the Protection of Journalists, the office prosecuted only two people.
The vast majority of thousands of criminal cases each year involve drug trafficking and organized crime, so it’s rarely enough to bring the killers of journalists to justice.
In Riodoce, they insisted on reporting the violence in Sinalo, although they were already heartbroken, although the land had been bruised.
Ibarra said there would be little use if there had been no information about the killers and no access to justice.
“It’s stupid to waste your time attending seminars like this.
“As long as we don’t know why, you don’t trust anyone.”
On the morning of May 15, Valdez left the Riodoce office in Culiacan.
Just a few blocks of his red Toyota Corolla was stopped by the two men; they forced him to get out of the car and fired 12 shots, guessing because of the newspaper’s name—translated as “Twelve Rivers.”
The gangsters drove his car away and smashed it in the vicinity.
Valdez is 50 years old.
His wife and two adult children were left behind.
Kulycan is talking nonsense.
But there’s actually nothing about Valdez except silence.
Since Guzmmann’s arrest last year and extradition to the U.S. in January, Sinalo has become the country’s bloodiest battleground, where rival forces are crazed.
Every day on the streets of Sinaro, one or a few people have been shot, and the cemetery is filled with the ornate two-story mausoleum of drug lord, which has become commonplace.
For the time being to forget the “calm” state is a time when there is only a cartel that controls this coastal state and its ports and drug-trafficking routes to the United States.
Even so, even though Valdez was acutely aware of the job’s dangers, Ismael Poholquin, a 60-year-old co-founder and director of Riodoce, felt guilty for not protecting his friends.
He believed that two mistakes had caused the tragedy.
In the first place, an interview with Damaso López was published in February, in which he was leading a cartel that battled Guzmán’s sons.
The report may have irritated the sons, who were bought out by suspected gang members as soon as they were sent to newsstands.
The second mistake was not forcing Valdez to leave Mexico for its own safety after another newspaper covering the same story was confiscated.
Vardez is a legend in Mexico and abroad, and his death is of great significance to the violence against journalists in Mexico.
His spirit endures, and his friends and colleagues believe that he has always been with us.
In 2003, he was a senior reporter at Noroeste, when he co-founded Riodoce with five of his co-workers and sold a $50 stake.
Pohaacqui said that in the State of Sinalo, “it is impossible to step foot in journalism without dealing with the drug problem”.
With the passage of time, the newspaper gained a good reputation for bold and honest reporting, and sales and advertising also increased.
Correspondence reporters like to be able to publish investigative reports without worrying about censorship, and readers also like newspapers to publish stories that others are afraid to report.
"Riodoce" five years after its inception, won the Latin American Maria Moskapo Journalism Award.
In the same year, the Association for the Protection of Correspondence awarded Valdez the International Press Freedom Award in recognition of his courage in the pursuit of the most authentic Mexican drug reports.
He admits he’s scared too.
At a time when the Association for the Protection of Correspondence presented the award, he said, “I want to continue to live.”
Later, in late October, he said in an interview with the local television station Rompeviento TV that "drug trafficking is a way of life in Sinaro.
As a journalist, you have to take on the task that falls on your shoulders—either by pretending to be deaf and dumb.
“I don’t want to be asked, ‘What have you done in the face of so many deaths... why aren’t you speaking out about what’s going on?’”
"Riodoce" all the staff miss Valdez, miss him like a pier worker cursing, miss the friend who has never shuddered for hugs and advice, miss the teacher who knows how to live.
They depend on their habits.
He used to wear that hat.
The El Guayabo bar, which is often opposite the office, is always seated at the same table.
Now, they’ll ask: was it his daily habits that caused his fall?
His death also made them have to think about how to do a good job and survive.
“They don’t like the women and kids you’re getting into trouble with the women and children you don’t like you’re affecting their legitimate company and the secret airport used to transport drugs,” Poholique said.
It's all beyond their bottom line."
As a result, maintain a high level of self-censorship and self-preservation, even in the safest of times.
Trust your own intuition.
If you smell danger, stay away.
Riodoce editor Andrés Villarill said that the problem was that “breath is a feeling of being deluded . . and then something like Havel will happen”.
He and the others have said that the old rules no longer apply to the states of Sinalo — just as it no longer applies to Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Guerrero, and other states, where smuggling routes are lucrative, and institutions are skeetmatic, government officials are corrupt.
The situation is constantly changing.
Cell phones were suddenly not allowed in the room where Riodoce employees attended security training; a few days ago, spyware purportedly sold only to the government had been used in Mexico to spy on journalists and activists.
On the outside, two police officers took a cold ride in the shade to escape the 104-degree (40 degrees Celsius) heat.
They were sent by the state government to protect Riodoce’s offices, which are housed in a four-story building in the middle-class district of Kulycan.
Some journalists half-jokingly teased that they didn’t know if the police would belong to 50 per cent of what the governor himself said.
It’s been a few months since the journalists went to the mountains, where drug cartels are under actual control.
This week’s Riodoce focused on three major events.
Among them is the murder of the brother of the great former boxer Julio Cesar Chávez of Sinaro.
There are also government funding disclosures in the governor's hometown,
As well as the kidnapping in an expensive restaurant in Kulyacan, which is only a block from the prosecutor’s office.
There is no official information about the victims of the abductions and the abduction process, so they need to be careful when writing about what the city is aware of: this restaurant is a favorite place for both drug dealers and officials.
A reporter learned from the public archives that the restaurant was registered in the name of a politician of the ruling Institutional Revolutionary Party (PRI), which dominated the various political strata for almost the entire previous discipline.
More recently, a number of PRI governors have been accused of embezzlement in a number of high-profile cases.
Verarier asked reporters to write about what had happened at the site of the incident, including a military attack on the son of Guzmán, Mexico’s leading drug lord.
Just a few months ago, readers would have reviewed Valdez’s column for the most powerful information about the kidnapping.
The 46-year-old Villarreal is known as a “skinny man” because of his thin stature, saying that “before, we already knew what was going on.”
At present, all our sources of information communication channels have been cut off.
Valdez’s office is now being used to store signs and stickers to protest the killing of journalists, as the employees became what they never wanted to be: activists on behalf of the media.
The next morning, journalist Miriam Ramirez went out of the room with a logo and went to the local prosecutor’s office for a march related to another journalist.
Salvador Adame disappeared three days after he was killed in the western state of Michoacán.
Officials found a charred body and said that according to the DNA test, it was his body.
Across the country, journalists have become more outspoken as they organize protests, smearing the words “rescue media” on sidewalks and buildings.
On this day, Director Poholique is not based in Washington, and he is trying to get justice and international support for the Valdez case.
The day after Valdez’s death, in a meeting with the governor of Sinaro, Ramirez accused the authorities of spying on journalists and killing them because they told the facts.
In view of the fact that her anger has affected her objectivity, she has also called for resigning from her position covering government affairs.
Vardez has repeatedly reiterated that journalists in Mexico are “surrounded” by organized crime, complicity in government officials and indifferential societies.
In his previous book, Drug Journalism, he wrote that journalists were not only mutilated by drug cartels, but also on a list of politicians and security forces complicity with organized crime.
Media watchdog group Articulo 19 blamed police and public officials for more than half of last year’s journalist attacks.
Ramirez said, "In Mexico, they're going to kill you just to shut you up."
It’s clear that these murders are chilling.
No one has forgotten that six years ago blogger Maria Elisabeth Marcias died on the northern border of Tamaulipas.
Her body was accompanied by a note purportedly signed by Zetas cartels: “I am here for my reports.”
Her head was cut off with a computer keyboard and headphones next to it.
After Miroslava Brech was killed in March, some newspapers chose to close, such as the El Norte newspaper on the northern border of Chihuahua.
El Manana of Nuevo Laredo’s directors was killed in 2014, but they continued.
In 2010, Diario de Ciudad Juarez used a front-page piece of news to talk openly about drug cartels with the headline “What do you want from us?”
Some of the journalists fled their homeland or even left the country.
It was a difficult choice.
It is difficult to find work in exile, and they still need to be cautious and alert to danger in the streets.
At times, they will be arrested, and it is said that photographer Ruben Espinosa was killed in a Mexican city with four women three months after fleeing Veracruz three months after fleeing Veracruz in 2015.
It’s a day-to-day step for those who stay behind the scenes and continue to work.
Ibarra, who wanted to be apocalypse, admitted he was horrified by reporting on the drug trade.
“Mexican is turning into hell, so I’ve become a journalist,” he said.
In the middle-of-the-night last Friday, when the latest issue of the newspaper had been printed, Riodoce’s editors were sitting in the aisle outside the office drinking beer when suddenly their cell phones rang.
A series of gunfights between gang rival forces and security forces near the beach resort city of Mazatlán have confirmed the deaths of 19 people.
The fighting is also escalating, as a series of cartel messages found in the region foreshadowed.
On the side of the road, they uploaded the news to Riodoce’s website via their mobile phones.
The next day’s headlines had to be changed.
The siren blared in the neighborhood—another life-and-death duel gunfight took place in the area.
Poholique glanced at the policemen who were standing guard there to see if they were alert.
If they’re a little scared, they don’t show it either.
The newspaper’s founder had been killed, and in his large portrait, his middle finger pointed to the whole of Sinalo, where the entire staff of Riodoce had followed in his footsteps.
On the same day Havel was killed, the intern asked me to send her into the street to participate in the report.
The size of China's self-driving tourism in 2020 may exceed one trillion yuan
[Abstract] In the context of consumption upgrading and the prevalence of free travel, self-driving travel is encouraged by national policies and loved by more and more consumers.
But at the same time, the self-driving travel market, which is expected to break through the trillion scale, is also facing dual challenges.
In the context of consumption upgrading and the prevalence of free travel, self-driving travel is encouraged by national policies and loved by more and more and more consumers.
But at the same time, it is expected to break through the trillion-scale self-driving tourism market is also facing a double challenge, on the one hand, the development of the self-driving travel industry and market demand is not directly proportional, self-driving tour clubs and travel agencies and other self-driving tour organizers are facing the dilemma of meager profits; on the other hand, the infrastructure and services faced by self-driving travel are still in the initial stage.
It accounts for 60% of the total number of domestic tourists, and the size of the market is expected to break trillions.
In the whole year of 2016, 4.44 billion domestic tourism, self-driving travel accounted for about 60% of the total number of domestic tourism trips, by 2020, the size of the self-driving tourism market is expected to exceed trillion.
Judging from the statistics on the number of self-driving car trips by the China Association of tourist vehicles and ships, the number of tourists has continued to grow in the past five years, and the proportion of tourists in domestic tourists has stabilized at more than half.
At the same time, various regions, as well as at the national level, have shown more attention and support for the emerging field of self-driving travel, and from 2016 to 2017, the policies and regulations related to self-driving cars and camping tourism were intensively introduced.
In July 2017, eight departments such as the National Tourism Administration and the State General Administration of Sports jointly issued the Development Plan for Automobile Self-Driving Sports Camps; in the same month, 14 departments such as the National Development and Reform Commission and the Ministry of Finance jointly issued the Action Plan for Promoting the Development and Quality Improvement of Rural Tourism (2017).
The introduction of policies has provided opportunities and policy guidance for the development of domestic self-driving tourism.
The self-driving tour club makes meager profits, and the complete industrial chain is the key.
Despite the huge potential of the domestic self-driving tourism market and the active guidance of national policies, the self-driving tourism industry is still in the initial stage.
According to the annual development report of China's self-driving tour in 2016-2017, the domestic self-driving tour clubs, car clubs, car clubs, etc. have reached thousands, but the proportion of self-guided self-driving travel of tourists accounts for the largest part, and the proportion of tourists who participate in various institutions such as car clubs and self-driving clubs accounts for only about 15%.
At the same time, self-developed self-guided self-driving travel products online tourism enterprises and traditional travel agencies are also a handful, a lot of online travel platforms are only sold by some suppliers to provide self-driving travel products, and some self-driving tour clubs are also important suppliers.
In terms of organizing self-driving tourism activities, it covers many aspects of "eating, living, traveling, traveling, shopping and entertainment", with a high degree of fragmentation, heavy upstream supply chain, and large random consumption by tourists.
Li Guanjie, founder and CEO Li Guanjie, believes that the self-driving tour club is similar in nature to the travel agency, and the main profit model in the early stage is based on offline activities, and the core point is self-driving tour line products and leadership services.
However, the homogenization of line products in the field of self-driving travel is serious, the service level of the leader is uneven, and the self-driving tour club is facing the need to transform again.
It can be seen that the self-driving tour itself is not an independent industry, but it forms a cross-regional and industrial ecological circle with the displacement characteristics of its self-driving tour, forming a complete industrial chain is very important.
Experts pointed out that in addition to basic tourism consumption, experience is a more important consumer element.
Therefore, for the development of the self-driving travel industry, we should enhance the characteristics of the experience, develop the theme of "self-driving travel +" and the IP of self-driving travel products.
Scenic lane is not only the road, the car camp should not only meet the accommodation
In the face of the rise of self-driving travel, the basic supporting development of self-driving travel is also relatively primitive.
Li Guanjie pointed out that the tourism scenic road is not just a road, but needs to integrate the elements of transportation and tourism, but the construction of highways and tourism itself is the responsibility of the two departments, the transportation department to increase the elements of tourism, but also stay in the construction of toilets, consulting centers, publicity columns and other facilities, and the combination of tourism is still not high.
Compared with the well-known self-driving route "U.S. Highway 66", the construction experience is worthy of reference.
Scenic road design is no longer the most direct and quick path between the starting point to the destination, but follows the design concept of "experiencing driving fun" to provide self-guided tourists with the best enjoyment of the beautiful scenery along the road; the scenic road can be linked to the point and face-to-face tourist landscape, which not only retains the historical value and cultural value of eco-tourism resources along the route, and can also hold a variety of thematic activities to enhance the participatory and leisure experience of tourists and create economic benefits for the local tourism industry.
In addition to scenic roads, the camp is also an important supporting facility for self-driving trips.
However, there are not many self-driving tourists who choose to stay in the camp.
They believe that a lot of camps are biased towards infrastructure, which can only meet the needs of eating and living, the grade is not high, and the single product cannot meet the needs of self-driving travel, which contains a lot of elements.
Some industry insiders who did not want to be named said that in the past few years, a lot of the enterprises that did the camp were in order to take the land, after taking the land, the supporting infrastructure construction and services in the later period did not keep up, and the operating conditions were a lot of losses, and without the late capital injection, it was difficult to do some corresponding supporting products.
The consumer group
According to the data released by the China Tourism Vehicle and Boat Association in 2017, from the age point of view, the self-driving travel group is concentrated in 21-40 years old, of which 31-40 years are mainly, this part of the population has a certain economic foundation, and the demand for personalized tourism is relatively high.
Judging from the accommodation survey, self-guided people prefer non-standard accommodation.
From the point of view of self-driving types, the surrounding self-driving is the most important type of self-driving tour, accounting for 84.32%.
Self-driving is the desire of a lot of people, but the proportion is still relatively small.
At the same time, with the improvement of consumer demand, in the past, tourists who like to "visit the attractions" began to change the concept, no longer in the process of self-guided travel "to get out of the car to take pictures, get on the bus to sleep", but to pay more attention to the experience and process of travel.
Dinosaurs used camouflage to hide from the enemy.
At first glance, it’s scary, but it looks like a statue.
The researchers published the first results of an inconceivably rhino-sized, rhino-sized fossil discovered by Canadian miners, pointing out that the fossil is well-preserved, so experts call it the “Mona Lisa of the dinosaur world.”
The fossil is so well-preserved that it was able to conclude that its squamous skin was red-brown in color. This suggests that it had some "troubled experiences".
It has been described as “the best-preserved armored dinosaur ever discovered and one of the world’s best dinosaur specimens.”
The fossil is a newly discovered nocturnal dragon species that lived in the middle of the Cretaceous between 110 million and 212 million years ago.
Its extremely well-maintained state shocked the researchers, who described it as “really breathtaking.”
According to the "National Geographic magazine" reported that the herbivorous armor dinosaur fossil is the best-preserved dinosaur fossil of its kind ever found.
On March 21, 2011, Shawn Funk discovered the fossil while mining at the Smytel Millennium Mine near Fort McMurray in northern Alberta, Canada.
He came across something that didn’t seem like the rocks around him, so he decided to take a closer look.
Later, the fossils he found were sent to the Royal Tyrrell Paleontology Museum in Canada.
It took six years for the researchers to reveal the beast hidden inside a 2,500-pound (1,100-kilogram) cavity.
After such hard work, the final results can now be announced.
“If you look at it, you almost believe it’s going to sleep,” said Caleb Brown, a scientist at the Royal Tyrrell Museum and lead author. The creature is on display in the gallery.
“It’s going to be one of the most beautiful and well-preserved dinosaur specimens in the history of science—the Mona Lisa.”
By looking at its skin, the researchers found that the herbivorous creature, though clothed in armor and like a walking tank, could be a major threat from carnivorous dinosaurs.
The armored dinosaurs used red-and-white camouflage to hide from predators, as well as a concealment known as “anti-shade,” a method used by many modern animals.
In this way, the experts say, it is better able to blend in with its surroundings, thus avoiding the enemy, suggesting that it was predated by larger dinosaurs.
The 110-million-year-old creature, a member of the noctridal dragon family, made headlines for the first time in May and is currently on display at the Royal Tyrrell Paleontology Museum.
After Mark Mitchell, a museum technician who was one of the team’s team members, spent 7,000 hours carefully unveiling the behemoth, the researchers are now studying it and naming it “Borealopelta Markmitchelli.”
The samples are extremely well-preserved, allowing Dr. Mitchell and an international team of scientists to record the patterns and shapes of scales and armor on their bodies.
They’ve found “anti-shadowing,” which is a common form of defense in the animal kingdom, which means that the creature’s back is darker than the color of its sides.
Although “antishading” techniques are very common, the results of the discovery are surprising, because the size of this species of dinosaurs far exceeds that of the existing animals that use anti-shading techniques.
This dinosaur is under pressure to be hunted by carnivorous dinosaurs, thus evolving in a favorable direction, allowing it to be hidden in confrontation as a means of survival.
However, most of the existing anti-shading animals, such as deer, zebras, or calves, are as small and fragile as prey, indicating that the creature faces real existential pressures.
“The frenzied predation of large armored dinosaurs shows just how dangerous Cretaceous carnivorous dinosaurs can be,” Brown said.
The chemical analysis of the organic complex in its scales also showed that the dinosaur’s skin color was reddish-brown.
Scientists are continuing to study the creature’s life cues, including the gastric content it retains to figure out the last food to be eaten.
They believe that the dinosaurs fell into the river at the time of their death, then rushed into the sea, and finally sank back into the bottom of the sea.
At that time, the climate in Alberta was as warm as it is today in southern Florida, and the rivers and oceans may be deeper inland than they are today.
Brown said: "This nodule dragon is really amazing, because it is completely covered with preserved scaly skin, and has so far been preserved in dimensional space, so that its original shape can be preserved."
As a result, the creature is now in almost the same shape as it was during the Cretaceous.
You don't have to use too much imagination to rebuild it; if you look at it, you'll almost believe it's sleeping."
The Cretaceous period was a time when two-legged large theropod dinosaurs roamed the earth.
Although Tyrann Tyrannosaurus rex was the king of dinosaurs, it only appeared millions of years after the pylon dinosaurs, which may have already been hunted down by some ancestors of Tyrannosaurus rex.
They consist of tall spinosaurs, a behemoth that weighs up to six tons and is 38 feet (11.5 meters) long.
The new findings, which appear in the Journal of Current Biology, are believed to be washed into the sea after the death of the junction dragon, after which mummies were formed in the soil.
On March 21, 2011, Sean Fink excavated the creature at a Millennial mine near Fort McMurray in Alberta, Canada.
It’s one of the world’s best-preserved armored dinosaurs, with skin and armor in areas ranging from the nose to the buttoes, according to the museum.
On average, it is about 18 feet (5 meters) long and weighs 3,000 pounds (1,300 kilograms).
It has two 20-inch-long pointed angles protruding from the shoulders.
According to the researchers, this herbivorous carp dinosaur was active in the present western region of Canada, and then was washed into the sea by a flooded river.
However, this dinosaur was buried at the bottom of the sea so that its armor can be completely preserved.
Fossil remains of this particular sample are so well preserved that the remaining skin is still covered with convulsions from dinosaur skulls.
As Michael Greshko wrote in National Geographic, this degree of preservation is as rudimentary as it is rare.
The more I look, the more I think it’s unbelievable.
Residual skin fossils are still covered with mottled armour on dinosaur cranial bones.
Its right-hand side is located on its side, and its five toes expand upwards.
“I can count them.”
