The total number of security personnel is more than twice that of the 2012 London Olympic Games.
In order to ensure the safety of the Rio Olympic Games, Brazil will launch the largest-ever security linkage plan.
Earlier, the Brazilian government said that a total of 85,000 people from the army and the police will work together, including 20,000 military personnel and 65,000 police officers.
The vast majority of military personnel will be in charge of defending Olympic venues, while the additional 3,000 personnel will be responsible for the security of international airports, subway stations and streets.
Police patrol other parts of the city, including subways, buses and streets.
It is reported that at present, 6,000 military personnel have arrived in Rio to provide security services for the Olympic Games.
In addition to being in charge of the four competition areas of the Rio Olympic Games, they are also responsible for patrolling the airport as well as the main roads connecting the airport and the Olympic venues.
