How did the activists learn to answer the question, “Who’s the street?”
On August 9, 2014, Brittany Ferrer was confused.
On his first trip to New York City, the St. Louis native was sitting on Airbnb’s rented bed and browsing Facebook on his phone.
She stumbled upon a high school student’s post.
“Police have just killed an 18-year-old, and the child is still lying in the street.”
She was puzzled when she found that no one else on the feed had posted it. She turned off Facebook and turned on Twitter.
An @TheePharoah user kept pushing dynamic times to her.
The St. Louis-area rapper live-streamed the death of Michael Brown, a unarmed 18-year-old black man who was killed by a white police officer in Ferguson, Missouri.
He pasted a picture of Brown’s body lying on the street, his body lying in the street for four hours.
“It’s crazy,” Ferrell thought.
But death is nothing new for her and her community.
She put down her phone and didn’t open it again until the same night to read a tweet about people gathered on Canfield Avenue.
There was police footage and pictures of people shouting, and a photo of a man who claimed to be Brown’s father, which read: “Ferguson police have just killed my unarmed son!”
She saw a message at the scene that a police officer was standing in front of a group of protesters with a barking dog.
In the other video, she said, another policeman raised his rifle and screamed, "I'm going to kill all of you ... the animals."
“It’s not normal,” Ferrer recalled.
“It’s not a typical murder that we’re used to.”
At that moment, I shuddered.
I'm angry, I'm crying."
She booked a return flight and went home the next day.
After landing at 11 p.m., she picked up her 6-year-old daughter from her father’s house and decided that the two would go to Ferguson the next morning.
Over the course of the next three years, what happened to Ferrer and Ferguson was the subject of a new documentary, “Who’s on the Street?”, which opens in theaters on Aug. 11.
It’s a story of survival and protest, love and loss, strength and tenacity from the perspective of the people who live in this neighborhood and are crammed into the boulevards demanding answers.
When Ferrer was driving to Ferguson with his daughter (wearing a floral dress with a crown accessory), the two rediscussed what they had discussed countless times earlier that morning — “about the black experience and the black condition.”
“Do you remember what I taught you when black people had to fight for their faith,” she recalls.
“We’re going to Ferguson now because it’s not right for the police to kill an 18-year-old.
“I had to take her with me,” Ferrell said later.
“What’s happened today is the climax of everything in the past.
She wants to know, see, and rise from it.
Not only does she need to develop well in terms of radicalism and organization, but she also needs to understand the different aspects of the black experience and the black situation and what we have to do in order to achieve freedom and liberation.
“Even though I was lucky enough to give her a racist experience she hadn’t personally experienced, she was no different from any other black girl.
“If I’m trying to protect her on this, I feel like I’m harming her.”
They arrived at Ferguson's first stop on a site opposite the South Florida Sand Road police station. The activists waited until police officer Darren Wilson was eventually unprosecuted, and the site became their camp.
Ferrer and her daughter are in touch with a local business owner who makes a bagged lunch.
They prepared the bags and were in charge of passing them on to the protesters.
Gradually, Ferrer began to form a new type of activism.
“My radicalism changed completely after Aug. 9,” she said, adding that while enrolling as an undergraduate at Missouri State University in St. Louis, she was the president of the Association of Ethnic Minority Nurse Students, focusing on food justice and health disparities in black neighborhoods.
“I don’t have organizational experience.
That’s how it all came.”
A few weeks later, Ferrer met the film’s co-director, Sabah Foleyan, during a nightly march.
Foleyan said that she and her photography director, Lucas Alvarado Faller, “just wanted to document” what had happened.
Ferrer had a question on his mind: “Do you want to record it or do you want to find a story you can use?”
This skepticism stems from many such cases: People—typically white people—went into a community to benefit from their pain and tenacity.
As Foleyan explained in a more recent interview, “We’ve heard that native people in Ferguson aren’t concentrating their voices, and we want to do it in a different way.”
Foleyan is already in touch with co-director Damon Davis, a regional artist best known for his activism against death row inmates.
His involvement led Ferrer, along with six other locals, to join the “Who’se Streets?” project team, and for several years following Brown’s shooting.
“This documentary isn’t someone talking or talking to us, that’s what we’re talking about,” Davis said.
“I’m mainly focused on how these characters are presented, because that’s the way I’m presented.”
While the goal wasn’t to focus on Ferrer’s experience — it’s to show that the movement was “not about savior leaders, it’s about community cohesion,” Foleyan said — it turned out that she was the most open of the film’s participants.
As a result, “who’s on the street?” surprisingly chronicled Ferrer’s personal lives, such as falling in love and getting married.
“I’ll feel naked,” Ferrer laughed when he first saw the finished product.
“But I understand that there has to be a certain level of vulnerability to this work and to humanize black people.”
In addition, Ferrer’s story reaffirms and repositions the role of black queer women in the broader Black Lives event movement.
Two of the movement’s three founders — who created #BlackLivesMatter on social media following the 2012 shooting of black teen Trevon Martin — were identified as queer.
“Black queer women in the leadership support the movement in general,” Ferrer said, “and that’s because we’re constantly fighting on multiple fronts.”
Black queer women have to endure all of this."
Foleyan agreed and mentioned that “the movement is really supported by black women and many black queer women.”
Because of her identity, Ferrer was chosen at the heart of the film. However, she insisted that "this is not an affirmative action for things."
“She’s the one who inspires that energy,” she said.
"Although she's on the front lines, it's not a coincidence. Because in life at these intersections, as a black queer woman, you have a lot of things to do.
Through the screen, you can feel her energy."
In Ferguson, meanwhile, news cameras have long since disappeared, but strained race relations between the community and the police persist.
Ferrer was given more than two-and-a-half-year reprieve for an attempt to seal off a highway during a parade.
She described the socio-political climate of what she called home: “It’s a mess.”
Despite this, since taking to the streets of Ferguson three years ago, she’s stuck to the hymns she’s trying to remember.
“It’s our responsibility to fight for freedom.
We have a responsibility to win.
We have to love and support each other.
We have nothing to lose, except the shackles on our bodies.”
