Similarities: The artist used Chelsea Manning’s DNA to create a portrait
There are several faces in the middle of the exhibition hall at the Friedman Art Museum in New York: white, black and brown, with almost imperceptible similarities between each of the faces, which are tethered by ropes drooping from the ceiling.
These 30 portraits were created by the artist Heather Dewey-Hagborg using the cotton swabs and hair crumbs that Chelsea Manning sent to her face.
Manning, who sent crumbs in his hair from the Levenworth prison for leaking classified diplomatic cables through WikiLeaks, was sentenced to a 35-year prison sentence.
The exhibition was opened on August 2 with the title “The Similarities”.
It's the result of two years of communication between Dewey Hagger and Manning, who has combined his disciplines with himself in technology, computer science, and the arts, while Manning, a well-igned dissident, has been imprisoned and has completed sex reassignment surgery in prison. Former U.S. President Barack Obama pardoned the remaining sentences three days before the end of his term.
Through algorithmic analysis of Manning's DNA, 30 portraits of "probably possible appearances" were created. Heather Dewey Harberger made a sharp and slightly merciless comment. His comments were both about the versatility of DNA and about the concept of identity itself.
Manning, who was born Bradley Edward, has publicly admitted to identifying herself as a woman during her puberty. He was convicted in 2013 on 20 charges, including six Espionage violations, computer fraud, and theft.
While in prison, her image was restrained, with only one photograph, a grainy black-and-white self-portrait that looked visibly unsettled, sitting in the driver’s seat with a platinum wig.
In 2015, Heather Deoui Harberger achieved great success with her 2012 project Stranger Visions .
In that work, she used physical evidence extracted from cigarette butts and chewing gum, extracted from crumbs to create 3D portraits of people who might look alike.
I just got an e-mail from Paper magazine.
“They wanted to interview Chelsea Manning in prison and they needed a portrait photo,” Heather Dowi Harberger told reporters at the exhibition’s opening ceremony.
Chelsea Manning couldn’t visit or take pictures, so they contacted Chelsea to ask if she would be interested in making a DNA portrait.
As a result, the artist and her imprisoned muse became improbable pen pal, exchanging many letters over the course of two years.
They've even created a comic book, Resilient Face, by Shoili Kanungo . The comic book envisions the president's granting privileges to Manning in the future, and she'll be able to see the exhibition in prison.
It may have been a coincidence that Mr. Obama called for her release a few days after the book's publication, when Manning spent his seventh year in a Kansas military prison.
In addition, the exhibition also has another deep meaning. Because in contemporary America, the Trump administration continues to declare war on government leakers and transgender military personnel, and at the same time to find new ways to osmote that can be hyped on Twitter to instigate separatism.
Asked about the special timing of all of this, Heather Deoui Harberger said, “There’s a reason for anything to happen.”
In an artist’s statement written on the gallery’s wall, Manning’s oversized signature is below (the “i” in the name is drawn with a single heart), calling for an end to “the paternity image based on gender, sexual orientation, and culture.”
On the opposite wall is her mitochondrial DNA sequence, twisting with a pencil that says the Cs, Gs and As symbols.
By containing 200 letters (or nucleotides, each artist’s complex scientific lexicons), Heather Devi Haaberg hopes to express, at least in terms of its physiological structure, by portraying these strangely similar portraits, that’s the case for everyone.
“I hope that people get out of this predicament, that our genome doesn’t care about who we are, how the published genetic data is interpreted, and how subjective it is,” said Heather Dewey Harberger, who is also on display in his 2007 video, “False Memories.”
“DNA data can tell us a lot of different stories, so that’s 30 of them.”
The exhibition’s last work, in a room, comes from a page in the previous illustrated novella.
It shows soldiers in the United States holding loudspeaker flamboyant, diamond-shaped Manning.
“When they make your speech stop, they win,” the wall reads.
“So don’t shut up.”
“The content comes directly from a letter she wrote to me,” the Philadelphia-based artist said.
“I’m just scared, I’m still going to say it.”
Manning hasn’t seen the show in person yet, as foreseen in the comics, but when she does, she’ll be greeted by a portrait mask of her own DNA, just as a group of protesters will be cheered on in the center of the pavilion.
It’s also for a purpose, like all the items in the exhibition, to the height of her genetic haplogroup information (5.2 feet, Manning’s height) written in pencil.
“In the past, I hung them against the wall, but I wanted them to feel like a group of people,” Dewey Hagerburg said of the portrait.
“But I wanted them to feel like a bunch of protesters following Chelsea, just as people were protesting to release her.”
Northwestern University professor and Oxford University employee wanted for homicide
Police said a man had been fatally stabbed in a luxury apartment in Chicago, where a Northwestern university professor and an Oxford University employee were subsequently wanted and were currently looking for suspects across the country, police said.
Cook County court records show 42-year-on-year-old Wyndham Lytham and 56-year-on-year-old Andrew Warren were arrested on Monday with a first-degree no-bail arrest warrant in connection with the death of Trenton H. James Cornell Duranlo.
Twenty-six-year-in-old Cornell Duranello died of multiple stabbings.
The Chicago Police Department issued a community alert that the body was found on July 27.
After receiving the call, the police arrived at the Grand Plaza apartment and found that there were multiple ruptures on the victim’s body.
According to police, Cornell Duranello died at the scene, detectives are investigating his murder, and police are actively looking for the two suspects.
Lessem has been an associate professor of microbiology and immunology at Northwestern since 2007.
Alan K. Cowbage, deputy director of campus relations, said in an emailed statement that Lyceem had now left and had been barred from the Northwestern campus.
“Now this is a criminal case, the relevant authorities are investigating, and Northwestern University is actively cooperating with the investigation.”
Warren is a senior finance assistant at the University of Oxford.
In an emailed statement, a spokesman for Somerville College Oxford said in an emailed statement: “We are always in contact with British police and stand ready to assist U.S. investigative agencies as needed.”
Warren’s co-workers were deeply shocked and hoped he would be able to turn himself in, the statement added.
Anthony Guglielmy, the chief liaison officer of the Chicago Police Department, strongly encouraged the suspect to surrender to the authorities.
“The police already have a rough idea about their whereabouts, and we are working to locate a possible location.”
Guglielmi said local, regional and national authorities were doing their utmost to assist in the search of the suspects.
Guglielmi said police received a phone call from Grand Plaza apartment manager, who had just received a secret tip that something had happened at Apartment 1008.
Police said the situation was terrible at the time.
Guglielmy said it was not clear if the incident also took place on July 27.
Based on security cameras, he said, it’s confirmed that Resem and Warren were in the building at the time.
“We are confident that Professor Lessem has a certain relationship with the victim,” Guglielmi said.
Grand Plaza’s management team said in a statement to residents: “Police are currently researching the victim’s criminal timeline and background and are investigating various motives, including a possible domestic incident.”
Russia's Deputy Prime Minister Mutko said: "We will complete the World Anti-Doping Agency road map in the near future."
Russia’s Deputy Prime Minister Vetali Mutko said it won’t take too long to implement the World Anti-Doping Agency’s (WADA) remaining standards to reinvigorate the rights of the Russian Anti-Doping Agency (RUSADA).
“Implementation roadmap standards have reached a certain stage where RUSADA has been given the right to plan and test in cooperation with the UKAD (the UK Anti-Doping Agency).
The WADA roadmap has been updated in this regard,” Mutko said on Thursday.
Deputy Prime Minister Mutko made the above statement when commenting on the second part of WADA’s road map to Compliance. This section sets the criteria for the restoration of RUSADA’s rights and was recently posted on the organization’s website.
According to Mutko, the standard does not contain anything that may be of concern to Russia.
He added, “Everything has to be announced and everything will be implemented.”
The results will be presented to the IOC [IOC] and WADA in the near future.”
However, one of the criteria mentioned in the list is that Russia’s anti-doping agency must publicly accept the findings by Canadian lawyer Richard McLaren regarding WADA’s approval of Russian state-sponsored dosing incidents in the country’s sport.
Commenting on the matter, Mutko said: “We are investigating and there is no doubt that our anti-doping system has failed.
All measures have already been implemented.
“But there is no national plan behind it, and we don’t recognize something that doesn’t exist.”
Moutko’s words were echoed by the head of Russia’s independent public anti-doping committee, Vittali Smirnov.
In an interview with the Russian outlet RSport earlier in the day, Smirnov acknowledged the Russian anti-doping agency’s problems in the past, but added: “We’ve said on various occasions that the report contains controversial positions and regulations.
No one plans to accept this report unconditionally,” Smirnov said.
In the aftermath of the doping scandal in November 2015, WADA suspended RUSADA’s right to carry out doping inspections in Russia.
However, in June of this year, RUSADA was given permission to plan and coordinate testing this year under the supervision of international experts and UKAD.
WADA Director-General Olivia Naigli, during a visit to the organization’s headquarters in Moscow in July, said RUSADA was “moving in the right direction” in an effort to restore its rights.
