To help you prevent the company from becoming the next Uber start-up
Amy Erret once wanted to investigate employee well-being at her e-commerce start-up, but the survey didn’t work.
Replies are often vague, unhelpful, and even worse deceptive.
Even though she promised anonymity, some employees still don’t trust the process.
Ms. Erret, the San Francisco-based e-e-commerce hair care company that manages the 75-person San Francisco e-commerce hair care company, said there was “a lack of consistency and objectivity in the responses received.”
That’s why she called out for help.
A number of new HR start-ups have sprung up to help companies understand the feelings of their employees.
They’ve built and licensed software specifically designed to measure employee engagement, allowing companies to quickly conduct polls for specific teams and population groups, provide employees with anonymous and complaint hotlines, and, in some cases, allow whistleblowers to bypass executives to report directly to the board.
“Right now, you can keep track of your health with tools like Strava and Fitbit, but where’s the company’s Fitbit?” said Jim Barnett, co-founder and CEO of Redwood City startup Glint.
Within three years, she used Glint to gain more insight into her employees’ thoughts and feelings.
In addition to rapid surveys and polls for a specific team, Glint offers company heatmaps, which units complained the most, and which managers approved low scores at a glance — which gave her insight into why.
Companies are beginning to realize that they must be skilled in controlling the company's workplace culture, so as not to become the next Uber. Former Uber employees previously published a blog post describing the harassment environment, and these spokespeople were punished by the company, after which Uber was mired in a scandal.
For start-ups such as Glint, the company’s desire for oversight is a lucrative business opportunity.
The global governance, regulatory, and compliance industry is likely to be worth more than $118.7 billion by 2020.
Convercent, a startup based in Denver, Colorado, is dedicated to helping companies prevent and spot bad behavior, and they saw an increase in interest and activity during Uber’s tarnished reputation earlier this year.
Convercent has nearly 600 customers, including Airbnb, Microsoft and Tesla.
Uber has recently signed up to become a customer.
Like Glint, Convercent allows companies to send customized “diagnosis” surveys, collect confidential responses in real-time, and view heat maps of their problem areas.
Convercent also provides an anonymous SMS hotline that allows employees to report bad behavior.
If the chairman is implicated, the complaint will go directly to the board.
Patrick Quinlan, Convercent’s founder and CEO, said: “The court of public opinion has usurped the power of the regulator.
“If a company is found to treat its employees poorly or behave unethically, even if regulators don’t step in, it could face high consequences of consumer boycotts, employee churn and embroiled in lawsuits,” Quinlan said.
With more than 25,000 employees across more than 500 locations, Ruby Tuesday has used Convercent for more than a year to make sure employees understand work guidelines and procedures and provide an easy way to contact the company’s headquarters.
Until then, if an employee wants to report a problem, they must find a phone number or e-mail from the company’s headquarters to file a formal complaint and hope that the headquarters will take it seriously.
It’s often a daunting and unsettling experience, says James Vettelano, general counsel at Ruby Tuesday, who doesn’t think there’s a good way to keep track of employee complaints.
But now that the company uses Convercent, it can more quickly identify and resolve issues that were previously unknown to executives in distant offices, cities, or states—such as discrimination, harassment, or unfair wage behavior.
“I’ll have a closer, 360-degree view of the work experience,” says Vitlanno, who runs Ruby Tuesday’s risk management team.
“We’re protecting our shareholders from employment litigation.”
Quinlan said that after the collapse of the Andorran accounting firm due to the Enron scandal, the companies began to pay more attention to ethics, values and employee engagement since 2002.
But it wasn’t until “social media came into its own world” that companies realized they couldn’t stop their revelations from going viral online.
“People see hope as a strategy before using technology to monitor ethics,” he said.
Both Glint and Convercent offer their software as a service and charge companies a recurring fee for the use of their products.
This business model and business opportunities are supported by venture capitalists, who are already supporting the two start-ups.
In February, Convercent raised $10 million in funding from the likes of Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital, whose total capital rose to $47 million.
In November, Glint received $10 million from Bessemer Venture Partners with a total of $60 million.
Given the interrelationship between companies, cultures, and venture capital, these investments can hardly come as a surprise.
A growing body of research shows that today’s employees have higher expectations of the workplace than before.
In a highly competitive market like Silicon Valley, well-paying water and interesting projects are just bets.
Employees want to feel that they are accepted, valued, and that their time is being spent on a company with a positive mission.
“When people are willing to join the company and feel that their voices are being heard and the work they are doing is beneficial, they are even more committed to the success of the company,” said Nina McCartney, LinkedIn’s vice president of global welfare and employee experience. LinkedIn uses both Convercent and Glint.
Investors make returns when their portfolio companies are performing well; the company performs well when its employees are loyal and committed.
When third-party analytics tools promise to increase employee loyalty and engagement, it’s no surprise that they’re looking for financial support.
Workplace culture experts say it’s important to have employee engagement data.
However, this data is useless unless the company’s administrators take action.
In fact, if a company requires feedback from its employees, it can be expected to make a change.
If the change doesn’t move fast enough, or doesn’t, it can lead to disappointment and cause employees to leave the company.
"If you're looking for 4,000 suggestions, you need to be ready to have 4,000 conversations," said Russell Lasse, head of consulting at business management firm Cot International. "He's seen mistakes in some companies relying too heavily on data collection.
“Because if you don’t follow up the whole time, the employees will think, ‘Did you really hear what I’m saying?”
“Do you care?”
“If you don’t care, why should I care?”
Erette said Madison Reed, who now conducts monthly employee surveys, has been able to take action on the same day Glint reported the issue.
Gathering feedback from employees about their frustrated decision-making speed and the quality of the communication, she was able to solve the problem by reorganizing the company's internal teams and increasing communication training.
Ruby Tuesday is acquiring new insights into its employees and, in the long run, they want to be able to make a transition in an industry known for its high brain drain rate to better retain them.
“If you’re not committed to creating a culture of transparency, you’re going to lose your talent,” Vitrano said.
“If you lose talent, you lose your customers.
“When you lose a customer, you lose the company.”
