The NCAA cancels twice-daily training to extend the Bears' daytime training
It’s close to 11 p.m., and UCLA football team lingers next to the team’s new on-campus practice.
Some of the players have taken off their sweatshirts, revealing their sweaty bodies after hard training on the first day of the training camp.
For a large part of the team, this idle few minutes is a break.
Players who take summer classes will return to their dormitory to prepare for the final exam after three hours of practice, and will return at 6:30 a.m. to the Wasserman Football Center to participate in the meeting.
“The next three days are a run-in for these guys,” Bears coach Jim Mora said as he approaches Tuesday on Monday.
The NCAA eliminated two daily exercises (designed to lighten the burden on players), but actually lengthened their day-to-day training hours — at least until the end of Friday.
That’s why UCLA’s first three workouts are scheduled to start at 7:15 p.m. to accommodate a schedule where the players are already crammed.
From rescinding twice-a-day workouts to doing more once a day, the Bears began training almost a week before last season, with Mora in Westerwood’s six-season practice for the first time at summer school.
They plan to begin 18 training sessions on Aug. 20, before they start preparing for their first official game in Texas in September.
Mora said that he supports the NCAA's rule adjustment, although it will add another program to the player's busy schedule.
“In three or four days, we’re going to give them a huge amount of training and it’s very challenging,” Mora said.
"They've also just passed. They're honing, and they're all great kids.
When I think about Saturday, we're going to have a lot of time, it's going to be great."
As soon as the training camp is over, UCLA ceases training. This is not an easy task, taking into account the neighboring parking lot and Luskin Center's clear line of sight. The Luskin Center Premium Hotel has been bruised by football thrown through the guard net behind the goalposts.
Starting this weekend, members of the Bears will be staying in the hotel. But Mora admits there could be a problem once the rooms are empty during the season.
“We’re going to take security measures,” Mora said.
“It’s a really worrying issue. It’s not far-fetched to think that someone might rent a room and sit in a room to record a training session.
It’s also pretty good if you have a training video.
However, we will take security measures there, and we will also take security measures in the parking lot No. 8.
We will do the best we can with existing resources.
I won’t worry too much about that.
“We’ve already made a plan.”
