Facebook will launch a fact-checking function to crack down on fake news
Facebook announced on Thursday that it will send more potentially hoax articles to third-party testing agencies and display the results below the original post. Facebook, the world's largest online social network, is trying to crack down on so-called fake news.
In a statement on its website, the company said it will start using updated machines to learn to detect possible hoaxes and send them to fact-checkers, and may display fact-checking results below the original post.
Facebook has been criticized for being one of the main propagators of so-called fake news, and many people believe that Facebook influenced the 2016 U.S. presidential election.
This issue has also become a major political topic in Europe, with voters encountering the proliferation of false stories ahead of France's presidential election in May, and Germany taking the lead in adopting support for the fines plan before the September elections, and if the social media network fails to remove hate posts in a timely manner, it will be fined.
Facebook said in a separate statement in Germany on Thursday that it was launching a new fact-checking test in the United States, France, the Netherlands and Germany.
In a blog post, Sarah Sue, product manager of Facebook News Feed, said: "In addition to seeing which stories are questioned by third-party fact-checkers, people want to have more context to help them make informed decisions about what they read and share."
She added that Facebook would continue to test its “relevant articles” feature and make other changes to its news feed to reduce fake news.
Creating a Graphic Novel: The Story of an English Castle.
More recently, the tiny Scottney Castle has become the main character in a compressed music video, and at one point served as a refuge for Margaret Thatcher during the 1970s and 1980s.
But Scottney has witnessed 700 years of turbulence in his cosy location in the Baylor River Gorge in Kent, and it is now a source of inspiration for a graphic novel written by Jamie Rhodes (a Yorkshireman who has a special interest in historical documents and keeps mops).
"You can see the handwriting of the ancients 300 years ago, and I think it's so beautiful," he said.
“What kind of personal fate do you want this letter to fall into my hands?”
From the Middle Ages to the time of the Edwardians, "A Castle of England" is a collection of families living in Skotney, and each story is written by different and future British artists such as Isaac Lenkiewicz, Briony May Smith, William Exley, Becky Palmer, and Isabel Greenberg.
This isn’t Roz’s first archive-based book: He also wrote The Teeth of the Dead in 2014 and Other Stories, a book inspired by documents from the British Library, a work he’s accomplished with an uncontrollable creative passion.
He said, “I couldn’t suppress my impulse to write when I saw the thumb imprint left on one side of the letter, written by an 18th-century double agent who worked for the Jakob school,” he said.
“That’s his fingerprints!
And the letters have always smelled of smoke, because at that time, you need to ignite a ignition in order to have a bright light.
For us 300 years from now, this smell makes people feel that they are also with us.
“Oh, I love the archives!”
For those who have been moved by the old piece of paper, it seems fantastic to have the support of England’s National Trust and the Arts Council and have lived in the castle for almost four months (“To be honest, I’ve stayed a little longer”).
Lodz spent a number of days exploring Victoria’s “new” castles, flipping through the archives and studying the estate’s many treasures and antiquities, such as hyena heads hung on the walls and bottles that had spent centuries in the moat.
He strolled through the land and ruins of the old castle, chatting with vagrants, gardeners, and sometimes the artists who illustrated his books.
Then in the evening, he'll "drink some whiskey and write."
Skotney’s secret history is not yet clear, as the National Trust was only fully established after the death of its last heir, Elizabeth Hershey, in 2006.
Ten years later, when Lodz arrived, the staff began to process the archives, and he described them as "hundreds of thousands of years ago the aristocracy is gone: 'Oh, put it in the attic'."
Diaries, letters from the Age of the British Empire, a map depicting the boundaries of various countries: “You would think that land disputes would be boring,” Rhodes said, “but two families will fight for a birch tree.”
“Leave it, guys.”
Some of the stories in the work use the castle as a starting point for a broader study of British history, including The Labourer , as a curvature of a man who left the newly built castle to take part in the Peasant Rebellion of 1381.
“A story called Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex chronicles a riot in the castle in 1380,” Rhodes said.
“Peasant insurrection began in that area, and I think it’s a good place to launch an uprising.
Maybe there’s a connection between the two?”
Other stories are deeply rooted in the castle’s history, such as “The Priest,” which tells the story of a Jesuit priest who hid a Jesuit priest for seven years during the British Reformation.
Or The Smuggler, the 18th-century Smuggler who is believed to have held his own funeral by filling a coffin with a coffin (a few years before his coffin was exposed to the ground.
“The Darrells” is Rhodes’ favorite of the Rottney castle family: “These gentlemen seem scoundrel, constantly borrowing money and spending money on things they can’t afford.”
Generations of Darrell men solve their debt problems by marrying rich old women, and I think they should be a group of handsome lads who know how to pursue a woman."
With the end of his mission in the castle, Rhodes set out to search for other bizarre writing experiences.
He spent two weeks in the wilds of Finland completing a graphic novel about “El?¤m?¤nm?¤ki,” an early 20th-century sanatorium that was slowly scrapped.
But Scottney had a special place for him.
“I’m sad to leave,” he said.
“It’s like, ‘Oh, I’ve got to face the real-world life.’
