In recent years, Silicon Valley giants such as Google, Microsoft, Amazon and Facebook have engaged in an arms race in the field of artificial intelligence (AI).
Sex robots are indeed on the rise, but we should be careful.
Just at a time when you don’t think 2017 will be even weird, the field of sex robots has released a new technology development report.
Oh yeah.
Nowadays, sex robots are a big deal.
More recently, the Responsible Robotics Foundation (FRR) has released a report that looks at how sex robots will develop over the next five to ten years.
According to Noel Schaki, professor emeritus of robotics in robotics and artificial intelligence at the University of Sheffield and co-founder of FRR, we need to start taking the rise of sex robots seriously.
Customers can choose the robot's eye color, nipple shape and nail type.
At the launch of the new report, Shakey said: "People are sneezing behind their backs, but the major companies have launched a lot of sex robots, and we're going to see a lot more."
Modern sex dolls have evolved from inflatable dolls into robots, they have silicone skin, feel very warm, but also deliberately use artificial intelligence to set a human-like sexual response.
Users can also “customize” their robots to suit their preferences, ranging from eye color and nipple shape to nail type or color.
But what’s even more disturbing is the fact that a lot of robots with “personality” can make “replicating your wife” look advanced.
Modern sex toys have evolved into robots with silica gel skin.
The RealBotix robot, for example, can come from a definition based on characteristics that the user finds attractive, such as shyness.
There’s also a Roxxxy Gold sex robot developed by True Companion that presupplifies personality traits, including the slightly timid “Cold Farah” and the “Holiday Wild Wendy.”
“Roxxxy Gold bots have the personality that matches you as much as you can,” True Companion said.
So she loves what you love and what you hate.
She also had small emotions like a normal person in those few days!
She can be languized, she can be talkative, or she can be “sexually enthroned.”
The rise of sex bots has caused a lot of uneasiness – not just replacing real human relationships – but servile femininity and convulsive rape cues on a lukewarm (reference: to resist) Farra are the most worrisome.
True Companion compares Roxxxy to a man’s vibrating rod on its website.
“If a woman has vibrating rods, then why can’t men have Roxxxy?”
However, these sex robots are not a simple fetish, nor are they just sex toys.
Their emergence and maturation point directly to the darker, deeper parts of our culture: an escape from perfect gender equality to sex with surrender as an additional option.
Of course, the majority of men do not have sex robots, and although FRR pointed out that sex robots are becoming more and more popular, they are still on the fringes of consumer culture.
But what permeates from the periphery allows us to see the course of our times.
A lot of people aren’t weird or aggressive until the free market gives them a chance to become so.
It’s no coincidence that these granular sex bots come at a time when women’s rights around the world are under threat, and there’s a president in the White House to brag about sexual violence against women.
The most chilling part of the TV series "The Handmaid's Story" lies not in the realistic picture, not in the hanging body, nor in the eyes that have been excavated, but the overly real imagination.
The Gilead Republic was a jump, but it doesn’t seem far away now.
The goal behind these sex bots is to create the bodies that are most similar to real women (albeit in the ratio of porn stars) to the extent possible.
The creators of these robots want them to have a human touch and imitate real human movements.
But to put it bluntly, their “personality” doesn’t represent real women.
They can’t break up, they can’t leave.
They have no recourse and no follow-up stories, no independence at all, and no possibility to break the illusion of outright slavery.
In other words, they’re sex slaves.
Or according to the popular saying of 2017: the maid.
