Trump's great achievements?
Mitt Romney is the epitome of common sense.
Political divisions have polarized U.S. voters, and, from a microscopic point of view, they have undermined American family harmony. It's no surprise in the Trump era.
Around Christmas and Thanksgiving, major U.S. newspapers filled their commentary columns with fear of reacting to voting for Trump’s relatives.
It’s generally assumed that we’re doing everything we can to puncture our “filtration bubble.”
Fewer voices are Trump’s ability to bring his family together.
I mention this because my cousin, a left-leaning poet living in Chicago who has long been at odds with the Republicans in our family, is coming to New York this week to visit me in New York.
She reminded me that uniting against a common enemy can have a powerful effect.
By working with Mr. Trump at the White House, everyone she knew, even relatives of Republicans who had been in a political squabbling for decades, was now suddenly and strangely on the same front.
It's certainly a good thing if it's all at the dinner-table. But I'm shocked that, from a broader point of view, there's a significant risk.
Although left-wing figures have always been instigated by Trump’s popularity, the ruting effect of his presidency has pushed us all to the right.
When we’re talking about “normalization” and Trump, what we’re referring to is the possibility that his quirky behavior might one day stop.
However, there is an even more worrying long-term scenario in which Trump wants to “normalize” relations with right-wing Republicans, who had previously objected to Mr. President’s standards and now suddenly appear to be a symbol of rationality.
I’ve found myself lately to Mitt Romney’s ecstasy and ecstasy a little nostalgic: putting a dog in a car, a 14 percent income tax (but at least we know his tax returns), and it really doesn’t seem that the crime of these two moves is too slight compared to Trump’s.
After the end of the Trump era, the next Republican candidate will only need to sensibly qualify for immeasurable improvements.
