China's household debt ratio is close to the U.S. What's even more frightening than not being able to afford a home is that you can't afford debt?
In recent times, everyone has been discussing the recent changes in real estate policy, is the real estate policy has been loosened again, the diversification of land policy, will the housing prices fall?
However, when we are worried about real estate prices, worried about macroeconomic regulation and control, and worried about the future trend of the real estate market, the People's Daily has once again written that the People's Daily commented on the adjustment of real estate regulation, and now the People's Daily has written about a more serious topic than buying and not being able to afford a house.
Let’s talk about a more serious problem than buying a house: repaying the loan.
China’s growing housing debt crisis
"People's Daily" overseas edition on January 17 published an opinion article on the "reward and loan...struggling young people's happiness and worries", according to the data in the article, in November 2017, the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences national financial and development laboratory, the national balance sheet research center released the "third quarter of China deleveraging process report" pointed out that the residential sector's leverage ratio continues to rise, from 47.4% in the second quarter of 2017 to 48.6% in the third quarter, an increase of 3.8 percentage points in the first three quarters of 2017.
The proportion of debt of the resident sector in the entire real economy has increased, and the leverage ratio has risen rapidly.
According to relevant research, the debt of Chinese households in financial institutions is dominated by consumer loans, accounting for about two-thirds of residents' liabilities, which is about twice that of operating loans.
Among them, among consumer loans, medium- and long-term loans dominated by housing loans accounted for about 80%, but from the incremental point of view, short-term consumer loans grew at a faster rate, higher than medium- and long-term loans.
In addition, China's household debt ratio only counts the total amount of credit obtained by the family sector from financial institutions, while Chinese families also have a lot of private loans, including borrowing from relatives and friends, and there are large-scale hidden debts.
According to the statistics of the Suning Financial Research Institute, the leverage ratio of Chinese residents in 1996 was only 3%, and in 2008 it was only 18%, but the leverage ratio of residents began to show rapid growth since 2008, doubling to 36.4% in just six years. By the third quarter of 2017, the leverage ratio of residents was as high as 48.6% in the third quarter of 2017.
In May last year, HSBC released a survey of nearly tens of thousands of respondents across nine countries.
The report was aimed at “millennialized” young people in nine countries (born between 1980 and 2000).
The report pointed out that China's post-80s and post-90s young people's home ownership rate is as high as 70%, far ahead of second-place Mexico (46%), which is also twice the level of this indicator in the United States (35%).
These data tell us a very likely problem, that is, compared with the inability to afford to buy a house, the largest potential "gray rhinoceros" at this stage of Chinese residents is expected to be unable to repay the debt.
Second, why is the housing debt crisis the most serious problem?
According to our previous analysis, although we have always said that China's house prices are high, compared with other countries in the world, China's housing ownership rate is actually quite high, so instead of high house prices can't afford housing, I'm afraid now to ask a lot of friends is whether you still have a loan?
Looking at the world's real estate market, China's real estate market started relatively late, in addition to what we used to call Hainan, there has not been a major real estate crisis, but just ten years ago in 2008, the origin of the world's financial tsunami was the real estate crisis in the United States.
The U.S. real-estate crisis is blamed on two rooms: Fannie Mae and Frederic.
At the golden age in the United States, real estate companies in order to expand the sale of the house with a very low down payment to low-income people in the United States at that time, when the U.S. economy is good, the real estate market is also thriving, but not long, with the U.S. economic growth stagnating, causing homebuyers' incomes to fall, so a large number of household consumer spending began to decline.
However, the current situation in the United States at that time is an integral part of U.S. household financial assets and real estate assets, nearly all low-income groups in the United States have real estate, and most of the assets in the top 20% of high-income groups are financial assets, resulting in the higher the debt ratio of real estate in poor families in the United States.
A serious fact is that these people are in a better economy to buy a house, so the payment of mortgage money is also calculated in terms of their better income, when the income has declined, the mortgage pressure is not only not reduced or even because some subprime mortgage plans have become fluctuating, so the real estate problems of these American families have gradually evolved into a real estate crisis.
Therefore, some people can not afford to pay the mortgage because the income is too low, so the bank began to recover the real estate, when the bank from the delinquent loan buyers to recover the property, they will be sold at a very low price, which led to a further decline in house prices in the region, the formation of a vicious circle, when house prices fell to a certain extent, more and more buyers found that the value of their houses is even lower than their own loans, so there are more people to continue to repay the mortgage, resulting in more bad debts.
Thus, a vicious circle is formed:
“The sluggish economy – the decline in income – some people can’t afford the mortgages – the banks are selling at low prices – the house prices are falling – more people are stopping the mortgage – the banks are selling – the house prices are falling...”
And the problem is more than that, because the house prices continue to fall, we stop repaying the loan to the bank after it is not that you have no debt, if the bank auction house to get the money is not enough for the bank's loan, then you still need to repay the money, simply say that you with a down payment of 600,000, the loan of 2.4 million to buy a house was worth 3 million, when the house price fell to this house is only worth 2 million, you no longer repay the house to the bank.
Therefore, in such a vicious circle, the US economy gradually collapsed, which eventually triggered the US financial crisis in 2008.
Therefore, at this stage, China's household debt ratio has reached a high level of 46.8% is a matter of great vigilance, this data is still lower than the level of developed countries at present, but the early signs of the problem have begun to appear, the country has repeatedly emphasized deleveraging from the beginning of last year, this is a very prescient judgment, the household debt rate will inevitably lead to huge risks, so we must strictly prevent the emergence of financial risks.
Therefore, since then, the house is used to live, is not used for fried positioning, the state has accelerated the establishment of multiple measures such as multi-subject supply, multi-channel security, rent-to-purchase and merger, so as to further resolve potential risks.
For our personal families, we must give up real estate speculative thinking, when buying a house to do their best, to prevent their own families from falling into a huge burden, only a reasonable assessment of their own income and debt level is a healthy family wealth development path.
Author: Suning Financial Research Institute special researcher, financial columnist, financial commentator.
