The British inventor implanted a chip in his own body and could enter the house and enter the car by waving his hand.
A British inventor installed a chip for himself, as long as a wave to open the front door of his home, enter the office and even start the car, becoming the first person in the country.
Thirty-three-year-old Steven Northam implanted a tiny chip between his thumb and forefinger, and is now offering the service to companies and individuals who want to simplify their own lives, a service enabled by his company BioTeq through technology.
He has teamed up with Dr. Jeff Watson, a senior consultant anesthesiologist at the Royal Hampshire Hospital in Winchester, Hampshire, to make sure that the implantation process meets medical standards.
This technology is similar to cat and dog chips and takes only 30 seconds.
Northam’s company will also replace the locks of various rooms and vehicles to be compatible with the chip, at a cost of £300 per lock.
The married father, who owns a child, has now installed the technology in his home and his BMW Z4, and he can start the car just by putting his hands on the steering wheel.
