The White House is costing thousands of dollars.
According to military records released by Time magazine, last month the White House's decision to transport a Marine Corps helicopter to the southern lawn to highlight the great glory of U.S. manufacturing, at a cost of up to $24,000.
Sikorsky VH-3D (also referred to as Marine One when the president rides) is an important exhibit in the White House on July 17 to highlight U.S. construction project activities.
President Donald Trump, Vice President Mike Pence, and senior White House officials toured manufactured products from all 50 states.
Exhibits include a Connecticut-manufactured helicopter, a Maine yacht, a Wisconsin-based fire truck, and a Mississippi forklift.
According to the records of the Marine Corps Helicopter First Flying Corps (HMX-1), the White House military office asked the helicopter to appear on a daily order (FRAGO), that is, only 36 hours before the helicopter landed on the southern lawn to participate in the "special" event.
The day before the event, the HMX-1 commander, in an e-mail message sent to the flight-commander of the superior Marine Corps, said, “I just want to make sure that you are aware of it, because this event is highly valued.”
According to this information, the helicopter will arrive on the southern lawn at about 7 a.m. on July 17 to participate in 3 p.m. activities, and will not leave after the lawn is emptied from 6 p.m. to 9 p.m.
This information also said that in the event that the departure time from the White House was delayed, a second group of cabin crew was required due to on-the-board time constraints.
The original aircrews were on standby at the side of the helicopter during the event, while members of the security forces stood nearby while the aircraft was on the ground.
Time magazine’s records don’t make it clear whether a second crew will ultimately be needed.
The Hatch Act prohibits federal employees from using government property for political purposes.
HMX-1 had asked whether the event had violated the Act.
“One of the questions we are asking is whether this is a violation of the Hatch Act and being told that there has been no violation.”
“Sounds like you’re locked up,” the vice-commander replied.
The White House defines “Made in the United States” as an official activity and is therefore not subject to the Hatch Act.
But even official events are politically charged.
At the event, the president promoted the Senate's issue at that time: health care reform, and touted the control of government regulations.
It’s no longer new to use Marine Corps One or Air Force One aircraft for political propaganda purposes.
Successive presidents have been required to travel on these planes.
But it’s not uncommon to expropriate them for photo purposes only.
Mr. Trump received attention in February in Melbourne, Fla., when the presidential plane glided slowly and ended up in the context of his first campaign rally, and his Twitter account was filled with messages of complaints about the former president’s use of the presidential plane.
Lindsay Walters, the White House’s deputy press secretary, said: “The White House is thrilled by the “Made in America” exhibition, which showcases products from all 50 U.S. states that are made from U.S.-made materials by U.S. workers.”
The HMX-1 crew regularly conducts flight training in the vicinity of the Washington area and on the south lawn, practicing the accurate placement of the president of the United States in the White House.
During Trump’s G-20 summit in Germany in early July, HMX-1 practiced 58 landings at the White House.
A U.S.-manufacturing flight was included in this month’s cabin crew training requirements, an official said.
The helicopter unit also flew an additional 11-hour “non-presidential support mission” that day.
For its part, the White House said the taxpayer did not bear the cost of any other items at the event.
A Sikorsky spokesman said that the company was not involved in arranging the South Lawn helicopter display.
The HMX-1 does not maintain a budget record for the president’s flight, so it is difficult to determine the exact cost of the event.
However, according to the Defense Department's hourly expenses records for fiscal year 2017, VH-3D's Department of Defense can be reimbursed at $24,380 per flight hour.
Troop records the plane’s 30-minute flight each time it flew from its base at the Marine Corps airfield in Quantico, Virginia.
According to the White House, the cost of operating the helicopter is much lower.
Walters said: "It is very misleading to estimate taxpayer expenses based on reimbursable expenses.
The estimated costs include personnel costs, maintenance costs, and many other sunburnt costs included in the annual allotment.
According to a 2015 RAND study, Department of Defense reimbursable expenses does not include personnel costs, but it does include fuel, maintenance, and contracting costs.
At the same time, former White House press secretary Sean Speck defended the use of the helicopter for the event.
“The idea is to show something made in the United States this week.
And I’m sure Sikorsky and Connecticut are proud of it, and they’ve both contributed to our national security, and I’m guessing there aren’t hundreds of jobs to rely on.
“And I think, like most Americans, we’re all proud of this helicopter and other military equipment, and it’s the hard work of countless Americans.
Of course, it's not out of place."
We don’t yet know if Trump interacted with the helicopter while touring the South Lawn at the event, but he did get into the driver’s seat of the fire truck.
Within the White House, Trump addressed Sikorsky company representatives and joked with the media about the company’s own plane.
He said, "I know Sikorsky planes very well. I've got three."
