He said in a statement on Wednesday that “the status of a woman cannot be underestimated”.
It is only when women are fully involved and included in all stages of the electoral process that there are truly representative and credible elections.
Afghanistan’s provincial assembly and presidential elections were held on April 5.
Intimidated by the Taliban into an electoral process that coincided with the transition from international military operations to the status of an adviser.
This year’s elections were the first peaceful transfer of power from one democratically elected government to the next.
Hayesom said that the importance of women's status will promote democracy in Afghanistan.
The Independent Electoral Commission released a report stating that 1.2 million of the 3.6 million registered voters were women.
