The UK Federation of Trade Unions (GMB) is focusing on investment in McVitie’s plant in Glasgow
The union hopes that the management of the biscuit manufacturer, McVita, will guarantee a long-term commitment to production at the Lasgo factory.
The Scotland chapter of the General Confederation of Trade Unions said the company had been silent about the future of investment in the Tollcross factory.
With more than 500 employees, the plant manufactures products such as Hobnobs biscuits (Hobnobs), strong tea biscuits (Rich tea) and so on.
Pladis, the owner of McVeteran, said it had invested more than £5m in the plant over the past few years.
He added that if there is a change in plant operations, employees and trade union representatives “will be the first to know.”
In a letter addressed to the company’s factory director, the Scotland chapter of the General Confederation of Trade Unions called on McVitas to “resolve the problem that funds have not been transferred to the modernization of production lines”.
Drew Duffy said: "We can't have a clear understanding of Tollcross's future business needs and investment plans, which is of great concern to our members.
Generations of families work here, and a high concentration of local communities and the Greater Glasgow labour force, is an important pillar of decent employment in areas with high levels of unemployment and severe social deprivation.
Therefore, from the point of view of employment, wages and economic prosperity, in the context of Brexit, the rising cost of living and the long-term decline of the manufacturing industry, there is an urgent need in the eastern part of Glasgow to continuously expand the production network.”
A spokesman for Pradis said: “The director of Tollcross just received a letter from CGT Scotland yesterday will provide an immediate response as we place emphasis on ongoing engagement with employees and union representatives.
Two months ago, in Tollcross, we held a regular meeting with employees and representatives of the CGT to discuss a number of issues publicly.
“We had discussions with our employees, and then, like all other companies operating in a highly competitive and economically uncertain environment, we conducted a review of our continued operating model applied to businesses and factories in order to remain competitive.
She added: “In recent years, we’ve invested more than £5 million at the Tollcross plant, particularly in terms of production lines and operational improvements.
We are fully cognizant of Tollcross’s historical position as a production site, and employees and trade union representatives will be the first to know if there is any change in our operations at the plant.”
May 11 (Reporter Chen Taobin), deputy director of the Sports Bureau of the Tibet Autonomous Region, Nima Tsering, said on the 10th that in the future, the garbage clean-up on the northern slope of Mount Everest will move from the public welfare action in the past to the normalized behavior led by the Chinese government and with the participation of the international community.
On May 10, environmental volunteers loaded classified waste into trucks.
In the past more than ten years, with the sharp increase of Everest mountaineering explorers and tourists, the environmental protection problem of the mountain location has become increasingly prominent.
In the spring of 1997, the Mountaineering Association of the Tibet Autonomous Region launched the first clean-up campaign on the North Slope of Mount Everest, cleaning up a batch of household garbage such as oxygen cylinders left over from the 1960s and 1970s. The video was also specially broadcast at the Tokyo World Climate Conference of that year, attracting the world's attention.
After 1997, the public welfare team successively cleaned the northern slope of Mount Everest, but the participants were mostly non-specialists.
Paris Saint-Germain warned Barcelona three years ago to sign Neymar.
La Liga may regret throwing an olive chees on Marco Villati
Paris Saint-Germain won’t give up on Neymar after making a £189 million offer to try to give it an exemption, but have they warned Barcelona three years ago to attack the World Record Raiders?
This sensational method has opened the summer transfer window, with the Ligue 1 club poised to break the world transfer record by more than twice the price of the previous transfer record. Paul Pogba returned to Manchester United last season for £89 million.
Once the transfer completes, Neymar will become the world's highest-paid player and the world's highest-paid professional athlete - Paris Saint-Germain will provide more than £500,000 per week after-tax salary.
Barcelona were angered by Neymar’s approach to the attack and the way out, with a source on the Catalan team’s board insisting the club had done its best to take care of him since his arrival from Santos in 2013.
But did the Paris Saint-Germain president make such a prediction for the world of football three years ago?
When City expressed an interest in signing Bryce Matedi in 2014, Nasser Al-Heleffi issued a stern warning to any club who wanted to sign Paris Saint-Germain’s best player.
“I’m not worried because they know that if they reach out to any of our players, we’re bound against them and we’re going to get in touch with their players as well,” Alhelefi said.
“We respect all the clubs.
“But if they’re trying to sign up for our players, then they’re going to have their own consequences.”
Earlier this summer, the Spanish club appeared to have crossed the line, with Barcelona throwing an olive pedestal to Paris-Saint-Germain midfielder Marco Villatti.
Paris Saint-Germain made it clear they would not in any way allow the Italian international to leave, and Barcelona are likely to be affected by the loss of Neymar.
